WEBVTT

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:04.680
(energetic electronic music)

00:00:07.813 --> 00:00:08.813
- Hi, everybody.

00:00:08.813 --> 00:00:12.403
Welcome to my talk, which
will be about rope simulation

00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:14.003
in Uncharted 4 and Last Of Us 2.

00:00:14.913 --> 00:00:15.746
My name is Jaroslav Sinecky.

00:00:15.746 --> 00:00:17.823
And I'm a programmer at Naughty Dog.

00:00:19.143 --> 00:00:21.433
So, this talk has three parts.

00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:24.963
The first is like, a general
overview of our rope tech,

00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:27.023
how it came to be, what
were the motivations

00:00:27.023 --> 00:00:28.943
and the features that we needed.

00:00:28.943 --> 00:00:31.173
And then we'll dive into the
technical side of things.

00:00:31.173 --> 00:00:33.673
And the second part will be
about a Dynamic Rope Simulation.

00:00:33.673 --> 00:00:36.523
And the third part about
Taut Rope Simulation,

00:00:36.523 --> 00:00:39.323
which I'll explain in a little
while, what that really is.

00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:41.106
So, the rope tech that
you can see in Uncharted 4

00:00:41.106 --> 00:00:45.963
and Last Of Us 2, dates back to 2011.

00:00:45.963 --> 00:00:47.953
We just shipped Uncharted 3,

00:00:47.953 --> 00:00:50.353
and at Naughty Dog after we ship a game

00:00:50.353 --> 00:00:52.903
there is usually downtime
of several months

00:00:52.903 --> 00:00:55.763
when people are free to
work on their pet projects.

00:00:55.763 --> 00:00:57.603
And I had this itch to
write my own solver.

00:00:57.603 --> 00:01:00.903
And I looked at ropes in
our previous Uncharted games

00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:03.333
and they had lot of limitations,

00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.166
and Position Based Dynamics
was already very popular

00:01:04.166 --> 00:01:06.713
at that time.

00:01:06.713 --> 00:01:08.222
So, I thought a rope PBD solver
would be nice little project

00:01:08.222 --> 00:01:13.222
for a few weeks, to hone
my simulation skills.

00:01:15.703 --> 00:01:16.573
So, yeah.

00:01:16.573 --> 00:01:20.193
After a month or two, I
had this rope sim working.

00:01:20.193 --> 00:01:22.903
It looked better than Uncharted 3 ropes.

00:01:22.903 --> 00:01:24.203
It had some bending stiffness,

00:01:24.203 --> 00:01:28.213
and was able to interact with
some simple collision edges,

00:01:28.213 --> 00:01:31.293
and with animated character collision.

00:01:31.293 --> 00:01:33.193
So, I felt pretty good about it.

00:01:33.193 --> 00:01:35.093
And just about then, Kurt Margenau,

00:01:35.093 --> 00:01:38.323
a lead designer at the time,
came to my desk and says,

00:01:38.323 --> 00:01:40.783
"We'll need some better
rope for Uncharted 4."

00:01:40.783 --> 00:01:43.073
So, I finally showed
him my little rope sim.

00:01:44.353 --> 00:01:46.653
So, that was pretty rare coincidence.

00:01:46.653 --> 00:01:49.783
That doesn't happen often
in a game developer's life,

00:01:49.783 --> 00:01:51.693
but I really had no idea
what we were getting into

00:01:51.693 --> 00:01:52.526
at that time.

00:01:53.932 --> 00:01:54.765
(waves crashing)

00:01:54.765 --> 00:01:57.063
- So, Drake was going to have
a grapple hook in Uncharted 4,

00:01:57.063 --> 00:01:58.135
and we wanted to set a new bar

00:01:58.135 --> 00:02:01.993
for presentation of grapple hook in games.

00:02:01.993 --> 00:02:02.826
We wanted something more early-stage

00:02:02.826 --> 00:02:05.333
than just a straight line.

00:02:05.333 --> 00:02:07.843
We wanted to have an actual bundle of rope

00:02:07.843 --> 00:02:09.953
that Drake would have at his belt.

00:02:09.953 --> 00:02:13.103
And he would reach for it, and
sling the hook, and throw it,

00:02:13.103 --> 00:02:15.673
it would actually wrap around the branch.

00:02:15.673 --> 00:02:16.840
We wanted to have slack on
different parts of the rope.

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.803
(Drake grunting)

00:02:19.803 --> 00:02:22.573
- So, we needed a system
that would be very flexible.

00:02:23.973 --> 00:02:26.723
The discretization of the rope
had to be completely dynamic.

00:02:26.723 --> 00:02:29.753
We needed to change the
length of the rope on the fly.

00:02:29.753 --> 00:02:31.283
And on any frame,

00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:34.083
we wanted to be able to
choose any number of points

00:02:34.083 --> 00:02:36.523
along the rope and keyframe them.

00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:38.683
That way, we could put the
rope into characters' hands

00:02:38.683 --> 00:02:39.993
and create these rope coils.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:44.093
But here, you can see the
keyframe notes on the rope

00:02:44.093 --> 00:02:46.473
are yellow, and the white are dynamic.

00:02:47.698 --> 00:02:49.063
And in the end,

00:02:49.063 --> 00:02:53.213
we settled on the maximum
length for the rope: 14 meters.

00:02:53.213 --> 00:02:54.046
So, I could already see

00:02:54.046 --> 00:02:56.063
that I was going to
have performance issues.

00:02:56.063 --> 00:02:58.393
I'll talk about it little
bit more in the second part

00:02:58.393 --> 00:02:59.883
of the presentation.

00:03:01.183 --> 00:03:04.163
And we created a system where animators

00:03:04.163 --> 00:03:06.603
could keyframe special values

00:03:06.603 --> 00:03:08.293
as part of their character animation.

00:03:08.293 --> 00:03:10.953
To tell the game what the
character is doing with the rope.

00:03:10.953 --> 00:03:12.773
Like, which hand is holding the rope,

00:03:12.773 --> 00:03:14.986
or if both, then which goes first?

00:03:14.986 --> 00:03:17.136
How much slack should
there be on the rope?

00:03:19.473 --> 00:03:22.293
But we didn't just aim for
high bar in presentation.

00:03:22.293 --> 00:03:24.663
We wanted to give
players also more freedom

00:03:24.663 --> 00:03:25.763
with the grapple hook.

00:03:26.603 --> 00:03:28.853
So here you can see, you
have a rope attach point.

00:03:28.853 --> 00:03:31.553
And you can drop down on
either side of the rock,

00:03:31.553 --> 00:03:34.993
and the rope will just bend
over the edge of the rock.

00:03:34.993 --> 00:03:37.573
And then you can swing along the wall,

00:03:37.573 --> 00:03:40.123
and the rope will slide
across the rock edges.

00:03:41.573 --> 00:03:44.393
And I realize my Dynamic Rope Simulation

00:03:44.393 --> 00:03:48.133
is just an utterly unfit
tool for this task.

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:49.593
I was already having problems

00:03:49.593 --> 00:03:50.426
with convergence of the rope, sort of,

00:03:50.426 --> 00:03:54.033
so, when the rope was close to taut.

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:58.213
And getting it to interact with
a collision in a robust way,

00:03:58.213 --> 00:03:59.793
so that we can have gameplay depend on it.

00:03:59.793 --> 00:04:01.781
And it just seemed really hard.

00:04:01.781 --> 00:04:03.718
So, we needed something different.

00:04:03.718 --> 00:04:04.551
(Drake laughing)

00:04:04.551 --> 00:04:05.703
- Something like this.

00:04:07.123 --> 00:04:10.153
So, here we started with
this straight red line

00:04:10.153 --> 00:04:12.633
that connects the hook and Drake's hand.

00:04:12.633 --> 00:04:14.983
And then as the hook and Drake move

00:04:14.983 --> 00:04:17.333
the line will collide with convex edges,

00:04:17.333 --> 00:04:20.853
and wrap around them, and
slide along them, to maintain

00:04:20.853 --> 00:04:24.143
the shortest distance between
the end points of the rope.

00:04:24.143 --> 00:04:25.543
And this is a completely different thing.

00:04:25.543 --> 00:04:28.743
So, this has nothing to do with
the Dynamic Rope Simulation.

00:04:28.743 --> 00:04:30.443
I call this Taut Rope Simulation.

00:04:30.443 --> 00:04:33.543
And the third part of this
talk will show little bit

00:04:33.543 --> 00:04:34.449
how that works.

00:04:34.449 --> 00:04:35.393
(Drake grunting)

00:04:35.393 --> 00:04:38.953
It didn't seem like a
terribly hard problem to me

00:04:38.953 --> 00:04:40.263
at the beginning,

00:04:40.263 --> 00:04:43.056
but it turned out I was
quite wrong about that.

00:04:43.056 --> 00:04:44.537
(Drake grunting)

00:04:44.537 --> 00:04:47.773
So, all the grapple hook
gameplay in Uncharted 4

00:04:47.773 --> 00:04:49.823
depends on this Taut Rope Sim.

00:04:49.823 --> 00:04:51.553
So, even if the rope has some slack,

00:04:51.553 --> 00:04:54.243
we always maintain this
invisible straight line

00:04:54.243 --> 00:04:58.993
that connects the attach point,
the hook, and Drake's hands.

00:04:58.993 --> 00:05:00.033
And this line has to always
stay out of collision,

00:05:00.033 --> 00:05:01.883
And this line has to always
stay out of collision,

00:05:01.883 --> 00:05:04.523
and it tells us how far can Drake go.

00:05:04.523 --> 00:05:06.003
It also serves as a safe line

00:05:06.003 --> 00:05:08.393
for the Dynamic Rope Simulation.

00:05:08.393 --> 00:05:10.813
We have these distance
constraints that don't let

00:05:10.813 --> 00:05:14.963
the dynamic nodes to get too
far away from the safe line.

00:05:14.963 --> 00:05:16.853
And as the rope gets closer to taut,

00:05:16.853 --> 00:05:19.163
then the distance gets shorter.

00:05:20.194 --> 00:05:21.103
(rope whipping)

00:05:21.103 --> 00:05:23.533
And so, for the rest of
Uncharted 4 production,

00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:26.163
all the effort on the rope
was focused on getting

00:05:26.163 --> 00:05:29.573
this Taut Rope Sim
working and robust enough

00:05:29.573 --> 00:05:31.384
so that we could ship the game.

00:05:31.384 --> 00:05:36.033
The Dynamic Rope Sim was
really, good as it was,

00:05:36.033 --> 00:05:38.533
since it was really just
visual icing on the cake.

00:05:39.865 --> 00:05:40.698
- [Drake] Got some big
plans for this winch.

00:05:40.698 --> 00:05:42.233
- [Jaroslav Sinecky] So,
another idea designers had

00:05:42.233 --> 00:05:46.543
for Uncharted 4 was to put a
winch on our off-road vehicle

00:05:46.543 --> 00:05:48.743
to create some new gameplay mechanics.

00:05:48.743 --> 00:05:52.873
And they wanted some pretty
long rope for the winch.

00:05:52.873 --> 00:05:54.703
So, they were coming to
me from the beginning

00:05:54.703 --> 00:05:57.583
and asking for a rope that
would be longer than 14 meters.

00:05:57.583 --> 00:06:00.363
I was always saying, "No,
no. 14-meter is the maximum,"

00:06:01.213 --> 00:06:02.273
but they really wanted it.

00:06:02.273 --> 00:06:05.303
So, they prototyped with
16-meter long winch rope anyway.

00:06:06.223 --> 00:06:08.673
Fortunately, the Taut Rope
Sim was a great solution

00:06:08.673 --> 00:06:09.833
to this problem also.

00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:13.663
The winch rope always has
a lot of tension in it.

00:06:13.663 --> 00:06:15.033
So, it was okay to say

00:06:15.033 --> 00:06:17.493
it will always be
completely straight line.

00:06:17.493 --> 00:06:19.143
No slack at all.

00:06:19.143 --> 00:06:20.693
And for the Taut Rope Sim,

00:06:20.693 --> 00:06:23.363
the length is not really a limitation.

00:06:23.363 --> 00:06:25.063
There's no regular discretization.

00:06:25.961 --> 00:06:27.723
So, the cost of the simulation,

00:06:27.723 --> 00:06:29.683
really just depends on
the amount and complexity

00:06:29.683 --> 00:06:32.667
of the geometry that it
has to interact with.

00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:33.553
(wind whistling)

00:06:33.553 --> 00:06:35.163
- So, we shipped Uncharted 4.

00:06:35.163 --> 00:06:37.873
And then we started
pre-production on Last of Us 2.

00:06:37.873 --> 00:06:41.003
And designers were looking
for some new puzzle mechanics.

00:06:41.853 --> 00:06:43.043
And they were thinking

00:06:43.043 --> 00:06:45.493
Ellie could carry a bundle of rope on her.

00:06:45.493 --> 00:06:48.603
And maybe she could just throw
it into the level anywhere

00:06:48.603 --> 00:06:50.293
and tie one end somewhere,

00:06:50.293 --> 00:06:52.303
and then go around, pick up the other end,

00:06:52.303 --> 00:06:54.313
and maybe climb up.

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:57.403
And it seems like we have
this great rope tech,

00:06:57.403 --> 00:06:59.983
so we should just be able to do it, right?

00:06:59.983 --> 00:07:02.703
And it was actually difficult to explain

00:07:02.703 --> 00:07:04.363
why we can't just do it out of the box.

00:07:04.363 --> 00:07:06.134
And why is it hard?

00:07:06.134 --> 00:07:09.943
The reason is that you
lose the safe line, right?

00:07:09.943 --> 00:07:12.471
Once you throw a bundle
of rope into the level,

00:07:12.471 --> 00:07:16.123
the Dynamic Rope Simulation
has to take care of itself.

00:07:16.123 --> 00:07:18.323
So, it has to behave reasonably,

00:07:18.323 --> 00:07:19.176
always stay out of collision

00:07:19.176 --> 00:07:23.213
so that Ellie can go and
get it back at any point

00:07:23.213 --> 00:07:24.770
and then use it again.

00:07:24.770 --> 00:07:26.873
But we decided that it's worth trying.

00:07:26.873 --> 00:07:28.493
See if you can pull it off.

00:07:28.493 --> 00:07:31.233
At least we limited ourselves to ropes

00:07:31.233 --> 00:07:34.013
that are always attached
at some predefined points.

00:07:34.013 --> 00:07:35.683
So, you don't just carry it all

00:07:35.683 --> 00:07:38.343
but you could throw anywhere in the level.

00:07:38.343 --> 00:07:40.593
And for Last of Us 2 production,

00:07:40.593 --> 00:07:43.132
all the effort on the rope was focused on

00:07:43.132 --> 00:07:46.263
revamping the Dynamic Rope
Simulation for a change.

00:07:47.113 --> 00:07:48.423
So, we needed it to make it robust

00:07:48.423 --> 00:07:49.663
so it never clips into collision,

00:07:49.663 --> 00:07:52.456
and the friction needed a lot of work.

00:07:52.456 --> 00:07:54.144
We tried to make it capable

00:07:54.144 --> 00:07:56.907
of sliding across collision features even

00:07:56.907 --> 00:08:00.303
if you pull on the rope very
fast, or throw it very fast.

00:08:02.483 --> 00:08:05.289
And we didn't have any
magic when we didn't want to

00:08:05.289 --> 00:08:07.823
have any magic, when bundling the rope,

00:08:07.823 --> 00:08:10.863
unlike in Uncharted 4, where
there is no time for that.

00:08:10.863 --> 00:08:12.543
So Ellie really meticulously gathers

00:08:12.543 --> 00:08:14.593
all the 14 meters of the rope into coils.

00:08:15.993 --> 00:08:19.233
We still use the Taut
Rope Sim when we can.

00:08:19.233 --> 00:08:21.673
It's there whenever Ellie
is holding the rope,

00:08:21.673 --> 00:08:23.223
it tells us how far she can go.

00:08:24.423 --> 00:08:27.213
It helps guide the throws.

00:08:27.213 --> 00:08:29.673
And as soon as Ellie picks up the rope

00:08:29.673 --> 00:08:31.903
we use the Dynamic Rope
as a starting point

00:08:31.903 --> 00:08:33.983
to quickly find the invisible Taut Line,

00:08:34.863 --> 00:08:37.143
which you can see
happening in the video now.

00:08:38.633 --> 00:08:40.093
So, it was a lot of work,

00:08:40.093 --> 00:08:42.243
but the rope puzzles
in Last of Us 2 are...

00:08:43.113 --> 00:08:44.373
It's a very unique mechanic.

00:08:44.373 --> 00:08:47.173
So, you can't really do
anything similar in other games.

00:08:48.253 --> 00:08:50.883
And the rope tech was used
for some other elements

00:08:50.883 --> 00:08:52.033
in both games.

00:08:52.033 --> 00:08:54.633
The weapon straps are
driven by a rope sim.

00:08:56.833 --> 00:08:57.666
You can see the enemy weapon strap

00:08:57.666 --> 00:09:02.666
has just like, four dynamic
nodes to make it really cheap.

00:09:03.063 --> 00:09:05.713
But it's actually enough
for quite believable effect.

00:09:07.584 --> 00:09:09.653
It was also for horse reins,

00:09:10.550 --> 00:09:13.550
(footsteps padding)

00:09:15.723 --> 00:09:17.143
- for dog leashes,

00:09:20.613 --> 00:09:22.983
or things like this chained infected.

00:09:24.352 --> 00:09:27.185
(chains rattling)

00:09:28.853 --> 00:09:31.013
- Okay. So, all these videos we just saw

00:09:31.013 --> 00:09:33.453
was to give you some context
for the technical part.

00:09:33.453 --> 00:09:34.303
It will come now.

00:09:35.623 --> 00:09:37.973
So, we can understand why we did this

00:09:37.973 --> 00:09:40.263
and what can be done
with this kind of tech.

00:09:40.263 --> 00:09:43.594
And now let's look at
how we actually did it.

00:09:43.594 --> 00:09:44.733
Before we start,

00:09:44.733 --> 00:09:46.223
I just want to say all the equations

00:09:46.223 --> 00:09:48.633
that you will see are here
really just for reference.

00:09:48.633 --> 00:09:50.473
So, you don't have to
worry too much about them.

00:09:50.473 --> 00:09:53.023
You should be able to follow
the main ideas anyway.

00:09:54.293 --> 00:09:55.963
So, the second part is going to be about

00:09:55.963 --> 00:09:57.903
the Dynamic Rope Simulation.

00:09:57.903 --> 00:10:00.033
And before we get to some
more interesting stuff,

00:10:05.563 --> 00:10:07.233
For more details on PBD in general,

00:10:07.233 --> 00:10:10.343
I will refer you to this
seminal work by Müller and Co,

00:10:10.343 --> 00:10:12.073
but you can find a lot of material

00:10:12.073 --> 00:10:13.473
around the web on the topic.

00:10:14.653 --> 00:10:17.843
So, let's say we have this piece of rope,

00:10:17.843 --> 00:10:20.503
but it's discretized into
a set of connected nodes.

00:10:20.503 --> 00:10:23.733
The solid black is fixed
and the others are dynamic.

00:10:23.733 --> 00:10:25.363
And in the given moment,

00:10:25.363 --> 00:10:27.973
we know the position and
velocity for each of the nodes.

00:10:27.973 --> 00:10:30.283
So, this is the current state of the rope.

00:10:30.283 --> 00:10:33.813
Now we want to move forward
in time with step ∆t.

00:10:33.813 --> 00:10:35.283
So first, we apply some damping,

00:10:35.283 --> 00:10:37.693
which will reduce our
velocities little bit.

00:10:37.693 --> 00:10:39.259
Next, we apply external forces,

00:10:39.259 --> 00:10:41.793
which will modify the velocity some more.

00:10:41.793 --> 00:10:43.777
In this case, we have just gravity,

00:10:43.777 --> 00:10:46.073
but we could have vit force for example.

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.773
And now we use these
velocities to move positions

00:10:50.773 --> 00:10:53.373
forward in time by step ∆t.

00:10:53.373 --> 00:10:55.973
And this will be the estimate
for our new positions.

00:10:57.573 --> 00:10:59.203
But the new positions do not look right.

00:10:59.203 --> 00:11:00.753
The rope has stretched.

00:11:00.753 --> 00:11:03.313
And also let's assume we
have this purple piece

00:11:03.313 --> 00:11:04.553
of collision geometry here,

00:11:04.553 --> 00:11:07.133
and the rope is clipping
into the collision.

00:11:07.133 --> 00:11:09.543
So now, comes the constraint
solving part of the algorithm.

00:11:09.543 --> 00:11:12.223
So, we will need some
constraints to prevent the rope

00:11:12.223 --> 00:11:14.623
from stretching and
clipping into collision.

00:11:14.623 --> 00:11:16.463
And since this is a
Position Based Dynamic,

00:11:16.463 --> 00:11:20.153
the constraints will act on
the position of the nodes

00:11:20.153 --> 00:11:22.053
and we will directly move the nodes

00:11:22.053 --> 00:11:23.633
to satisfy these constraints.

00:11:24.503 --> 00:11:27.693
So firstly, we want a constraint
that keep neighboring nodes

00:11:27.693 --> 00:11:31.743
at the same constant
distance l₀ from each other.

00:11:31.743 --> 00:11:33.503
And this format looks
slightly complicated,

00:11:33.503 --> 00:11:36.933
but what we're doing here really
is very simple and natural.

00:11:36.933 --> 00:11:40.653
So, for the first two nodes,
since the first node is fixed,

00:11:40.653 --> 00:11:43.073
you just move the second node closer to it

00:11:43.073 --> 00:11:45.113
to fix the distance.

00:11:45.113 --> 00:11:46.873
And for the second and third node,

00:11:46.873 --> 00:11:48.363
since they have a equal masses,

00:11:48.363 --> 00:11:51.773
we just move them closer together
by the same distance each.

00:11:51.773 --> 00:11:54.273
And then we do the same
for the last two nodes.

00:11:54.273 --> 00:11:56.913
And here we add the collision constraints.

00:11:56.913 --> 00:11:58.413
Collision constraint.

00:11:58.413 --> 00:12:01.013
We just means that we will move the node

00:12:01.013 --> 00:12:01.846
out of the collision.

00:12:01.846 --> 00:12:05.113
So, we will move it along the plane normal

00:12:05.113 --> 00:12:06.813
until it sits on top of the plane.

00:12:08.473 --> 00:12:10.123
Now, as you have noticed probably,

00:12:10.123 --> 00:12:11.823
by solving one constraint,

00:12:11.823 --> 00:12:13.403
we violated the constraints

00:12:13.403 --> 00:12:15.083
that we already solved previously.

00:12:15.083 --> 00:12:17.013
So, the rope is still
stretched a little bit

00:12:17.013 --> 00:12:19.213
at the beginning and
compressed at the end.

00:12:19.213 --> 00:12:21.293
So, we will need to go
over these constraints

00:12:21.293 --> 00:12:22.553
again and again.

00:12:22.553 --> 00:12:24.783
You'll have to iterate
until all the constraints

00:12:24.783 --> 00:12:26.133
are more or less satisfied.

00:12:27.723 --> 00:12:30.953
And how do we decide that we are satisfied

00:12:30.953 --> 00:12:32.583
and we can stop iterating?

00:12:32.583 --> 00:12:34.067
So usually, either...

00:12:34.067 --> 00:12:37.693
We stop usually when there
is some maximum error

00:12:37.693 --> 00:12:40.193
that's smaller than some threshold Ɛ,

00:12:40.193 --> 00:12:43.563
or when we reach a maximum
number of iterations.

00:12:43.563 --> 00:12:44.623
I can tell you that for PBD,

00:12:44.623 --> 00:12:46.873
you don't want to use
the maximum error area

00:12:46.873 --> 00:12:49.043
because if you change number of iterations

00:12:49.043 --> 00:12:50.343
from frame to frame,

00:12:50.343 --> 00:12:53.313
you're basically changing
the stiffness of the solver.

00:12:53.313 --> 00:12:56.263
So, you just want to keep the
number of iterations constant.

00:12:57.253 --> 00:12:59.223
And after, we found final positions

00:12:59.223 --> 00:13:01.083
that satisfy our constraints.

00:13:01.083 --> 00:13:03.423
So, we need to find
the correct velocities.

00:13:03.423 --> 00:13:08.423
And this is just done by
subtracting from the final position

00:13:08.563 --> 00:13:11.093
from the position on the previous frame

00:13:11.093 --> 00:13:12.333
and dividing by ∆t.

00:13:13.273 --> 00:13:15.723
And now, we have a new
rope state for this frame.

00:13:16.793 --> 00:13:18.253
So, that was pretty simple, right?

00:13:18.253 --> 00:13:19.783
And the fact that you manipulate

00:13:19.783 --> 00:13:21.603
the position of the nodes directly

00:13:21.603 --> 00:13:24.344
makes it very easy to
understand what you're doing.

00:13:24.344 --> 00:13:25.973
It's also very stable, actually,

00:13:25.973 --> 00:13:29.533
because again, as you
control the positions,

00:13:29.533 --> 00:13:31.853
the simulation can't really
explode as it easily does

00:13:31.853 --> 00:13:35.313
when you're solving for
some internal forces.

00:13:35.313 --> 00:13:38.313
But that's why PBD is so popular in games.

00:13:38.313 --> 00:13:41.413
So, if you need for
now, limit ourself to...

00:13:41.413 --> 00:13:45.123
Or limit our collision geometry
to just a flat ground plane

00:13:45.123 --> 00:13:47.343
and the constraint for
that can be expressed

00:13:47.343 --> 00:13:49.813
with this simple one line of code.

00:13:49.813 --> 00:13:52.703
What I just showed you can be
coded in one, like, afternoon.

00:13:52.703 --> 00:13:55.183
And you can get something like this.

00:13:55.183 --> 00:13:56.323
So here,

00:13:56.323 --> 00:13:59.313
the right end of the rope is
anchored and I let it fall.

00:14:00.333 --> 00:14:02.233
Ellie's just standing there for scale.

00:14:04.173 --> 00:14:06.533
So, that's not bad, right?

00:14:06.533 --> 00:14:08.473
But there are few
problems you will notice.

00:14:08.473 --> 00:14:10.133
One is that there is no friction

00:14:10.133 --> 00:14:12.443
between the rope and the floor.

00:14:12.443 --> 00:14:14.993
And when you look closer
at the rope on the ground,

00:14:14.993 --> 00:14:18.063
you can see how it bends
at these sharp angles.

00:14:18.063 --> 00:14:19.693
So, the rope has a resistance to bending.

00:14:19.693 --> 00:14:22.093
So, we would want to add
some bending stiffness,

00:14:23.523 --> 00:14:26.403
or bending constraints to
prevent these sharp angles.

00:14:27.263 --> 00:14:29.713
So, how can we create a
bending stiffness constraint?

00:14:29.713 --> 00:14:32.343
So, let's say we have these
three neighboring nodes

00:14:32.343 --> 00:14:34.833
with masses: m₁, 2, 3.

00:14:34.833 --> 00:14:37.403
And we want to straighten
up the segment of rope

00:14:37.403 --> 00:14:40.063
by moving them all along
the same direction.

00:14:40.063 --> 00:14:43.013
And for now, let's assume
you want to push them

00:14:43.013 --> 00:14:44.803
all the way into a straight line.

00:14:44.803 --> 00:14:48.763
And how do we find these
unknowns: d₁, d₂, d₃?

00:14:48.763 --> 00:14:51.173
So, since the targets are
supposed to be on a straight line,

00:14:51.173 --> 00:14:53.043
that gives us one equation.

00:14:53.043 --> 00:14:55.946
And then, we need to conserve
the linear and angular

00:14:55.946 --> 00:14:58.893
momentum of this little
system of three nodes.

00:14:58.893 --> 00:15:00.033
And that gives us two more equations,

00:15:06.653 --> 00:15:08.413
Why do I move the nodes in this direction?

00:15:08.413 --> 00:15:09.246
All right.

00:15:09.246 --> 00:15:10.973
I could move them in
some different direction.

00:15:10.973 --> 00:15:12.333
In fact, the most natural here,

00:15:12.333 --> 00:15:16.833
would be probably to pivot
m₁, m₃ around m₂ like that

00:15:16.833 --> 00:15:18.663
to straighten up this segment.

00:15:18.663 --> 00:15:19.496
So, yes.

00:15:19.496 --> 00:15:21.113
You do have a lot of freedom

00:15:21.113 --> 00:15:23.428
of how to create your constraints.

00:15:23.428 --> 00:15:25.093
The bending stiffness could be done

00:15:25.093 --> 00:15:26.723
in many different ways.

00:15:26.723 --> 00:15:28.993
I just chose this way
because it seemed convenient.

00:15:28.993 --> 00:15:30.513
And because I wanted to avoid using

00:15:30.513 --> 00:15:32.921
any trigonometric functions
to keep the evaluation

00:15:32.921 --> 00:15:34.303
as cheap as possible.

00:15:35.352 --> 00:15:39.653
But, the conservation of
momentum is an important rule

00:15:39.653 --> 00:15:41.543
that you need to observe.

00:15:41.543 --> 00:15:43.283
I know you probably heard
it many times before,

00:15:43.283 --> 00:15:44.663
but it's easy to forget.

00:15:44.663 --> 00:15:46.143
So, if you ever get the rope

00:15:46.143 --> 00:15:48.583
that's resting on its own like a snake,

00:15:48.583 --> 00:15:49.583
you probably have a constraint

00:15:49.583 --> 00:15:50.923
that doesn't conserve momentum,

00:15:50.923 --> 00:15:53.093
and it's adding energy into the rope.

00:15:54.583 --> 00:15:56.833
And now we do the math with
the system of equations,

00:15:56.833 --> 00:15:59.633
and we get this result
for the displacements.

00:15:59.633 --> 00:16:02.693
And each one is a multiplication
of the same expression h.

00:16:02.693 --> 00:16:05.123
And because we don't really
want to push the nodes

00:16:05.123 --> 00:16:08.063
all the way into the straight
line on each iteration,

00:16:08.063 --> 00:16:09.679
it would be way too aggressive.

00:16:09.679 --> 00:16:11.843
So, we introduced this multiplier 𝜁

00:16:11.843 --> 00:16:13.583
and that will be our bending stiffness.

00:16:13.583 --> 00:16:17.094
So, that's the value we
can use to tweak how stiff

00:16:17.094 --> 00:16:19.494
should the rope behave
when it comes to bending.

00:16:20.723 --> 00:16:22.473
And they respond more loose end to tie.

00:16:22.473 --> 00:16:25.209
So, we have coefficient
for bending stiffness,

00:16:25.209 --> 00:16:26.982
but the target for the
constraint is still to achieve

00:16:26.982 --> 00:16:28.123
a straight line.

00:16:28.123 --> 00:16:31.483
So, the constraint will still
be trying to move the rope,

00:16:31.483 --> 00:16:33.143
even just a little by a little,

00:16:33.143 --> 00:16:35.493
until it reaches a
completely straight line.

00:16:35.493 --> 00:16:37.573
The real rope up to a certain point,

00:16:37.573 --> 00:16:39.993
you can bend it without
resistance, basically.

00:16:39.993 --> 00:16:41.373
It has some free bending radius.

00:16:41.373 --> 00:16:43.543
So, if you wan to do
something similar here,

00:16:43.543 --> 00:16:46.038
so we introduced this 𝛼f
as a free bending angle

00:16:46.038 --> 00:16:48.173
per one meter of rope.

00:16:48.173 --> 00:16:51.673
And the equations are here
really just for reference.

00:16:51.673 --> 00:16:54.030
But the idea is that simply
the bending stiffness

00:16:54.030 --> 00:16:57.623
will only straighten the rope
up to the free bending angle

00:16:57.623 --> 00:16:58.883
and then stop acting.

00:17:00.443 --> 00:17:03.553
And now we can look at our rope
with this bending stiffness,

00:17:03.553 --> 00:17:05.303
bending stiffness constraint added.

00:17:07.713 --> 00:17:08.953
And we can see that it does not have

00:17:08.953 --> 00:17:10.933
those ugly sharp corners anymore.

00:17:10.933 --> 00:17:13.203
It tends to create these
more smooth curves,

00:17:14.143 --> 00:17:16.833
but we'll need to do something
about the friction, right?

00:17:18.113 --> 00:17:20.713
So, friction is problematic with PBD

00:17:20.713 --> 00:17:24.243
because since you're solving
constraints on position level,

00:17:24.243 --> 00:17:26.353
you don't know what the
constraint forces are.

00:17:26.353 --> 00:17:28.723
So, you don't know what's
the tension of the rope.

00:17:28.723 --> 00:17:30.383
You don't know what is
the force between the rope

00:17:30.383 --> 00:17:31.703
and the collision.

00:17:31.703 --> 00:17:33.253
So, that's one drawback of PBD.

00:17:34.173 --> 00:17:35.323
And because, for friction,

00:17:35.323 --> 00:17:36.533
you need to know the normal force

00:17:36.533 --> 00:17:37.623
between the rope and the collision.

00:17:37.623 --> 00:17:39.253
You can't really do that.

00:17:39.253 --> 00:17:43.043
But one simple thing that you
can do is just to remember

00:17:43.043 --> 00:17:46.313
collision constraint that
were used during the solver.

00:17:46.313 --> 00:17:49.313
So, where a node had to be
moved out of the collision

00:17:49.313 --> 00:17:51.993
and reduce the tangential
velocity at that node

00:17:51.993 --> 00:17:53.603
by some coefficient.

00:17:53.603 --> 00:17:55.603
And this is done after the solver,

00:17:55.603 --> 00:17:58.773
once you calculated the new
velocities for each node.

00:17:58.773 --> 00:18:00.853
And even though this is a
very simple approximation,

00:18:00.853 --> 00:18:02.393
it might be all you need.

00:18:02.393 --> 00:18:04.123
I'll show you how it looks when we add it

00:18:04.123 --> 00:18:05.523
to our little test case.

00:18:11.623 --> 00:18:14.383
So, that looks like a pretty
bit of the rope, in this case,

00:18:14.383 --> 00:18:17.963
and this is all we needed
for Uncharted 4, for example.

00:18:17.963 --> 00:18:20.183
Mostly, because the rope
never had too much slack,

00:18:20.183 --> 00:18:23.453
and it was always connected
to something on both ends.

00:18:23.453 --> 00:18:26.403
Unfortunately, it wasn't
enough for Last of Us 2.

00:18:26.403 --> 00:18:28.582
I'm going to show you these two cases

00:18:28.582 --> 00:18:31.733
where it fails to
produce billable results.

00:18:31.733 --> 00:18:34.703
So in this first case, the
velocities are reduced,

00:18:34.703 --> 00:18:36.963
and just a handful of
nodes that are in contact

00:18:36.963 --> 00:18:38.043
with the bar.

00:18:38.043 --> 00:18:39.283
That's just not enough to keep

00:18:39.283 --> 00:18:41.964
the rope from sliding
back and forth like that.

00:18:41.964 --> 00:18:45.193
It is a high contact force
between the rope and the bar

00:18:45.193 --> 00:18:48.104
due to the weight of the rope,
which means high friction.

00:18:48.104 --> 00:18:49.343
But we don't know that.

00:18:50.873 --> 00:18:52.853
And then, there is this other case.

00:18:58.053 --> 00:19:00.353
Where the rope just keeps
sliding and sliding.

00:19:01.785 --> 00:19:04.163
Yeah. So, for our puzzles it was important

00:19:04.163 --> 00:19:06.473
that if it looks like the
rope should stay up there,

00:19:06.473 --> 00:19:07.963
that it actually stays up there.

00:19:07.963 --> 00:19:09.793
So, we needed to do something
at least a little bit more

00:19:09.793 --> 00:19:11.893
realistic for the friction.

00:19:11.893 --> 00:19:14.673
And so, one thing we did is this kind of

00:19:14.673 --> 00:19:17.168
Static Rope Tension analysis.

00:19:17.168 --> 00:19:18.783
This is then, after the solver.

00:19:18.783 --> 00:19:21.233
And we look at the rope
shape and nobody touches,

00:19:21.233 --> 00:19:23.731
or would upset our collision,
and try to approximate

00:19:23.731 --> 00:19:26.543
what the tension, and the
resulting contact force,

00:19:26.543 --> 00:19:28.623
and the friction of the rope could be.

00:19:29.543 --> 00:19:31.923
And we use facts like, the
tension is zero at the free end

00:19:31.923 --> 00:19:33.273
of the rope.

00:19:33.273 --> 00:19:35.673
If the rope bends around
the collision edge,

00:19:35.673 --> 00:19:39.719
we approximate the resulting
normal force and friction.

00:19:39.719 --> 00:19:41.383
If the rope touches collision,

00:19:41.383 --> 00:19:44.173
we approximate the normal
force due to the gravity.

00:19:45.073 --> 00:19:46.623
If the rope is bending a lot,

00:19:46.623 --> 00:19:49.433
and it's not because it
bends around collision edge,

00:19:49.433 --> 00:19:51.893
we let the tension fall off to zero.

00:19:51.893 --> 00:19:54.063
And for a free hanging segment of rope,

00:19:54.063 --> 00:19:56.373
we just add tension
resulting from gravity.

00:19:57.363 --> 00:19:59.563
And so, this is all very approximate.

00:19:59.563 --> 00:20:00.033
It's completely wrong when
the rope is moving a lot,

00:20:00.033 --> 00:20:02.353
It's completely wrong when
the rope is moving a lot,

00:20:02.353 --> 00:20:05.283
because we don't take
velocities into account at all,

00:20:05.283 --> 00:20:08.997
but that's okay because the
friction is not important for us

00:20:08.997 --> 00:20:11.939
when the rope is moving fast.

00:20:11.939 --> 00:20:15.928
So, this worked fine for
us for the rope puzzles,

00:20:15.928 --> 00:20:18.203
but there was one more thing we had to do.

00:20:18.203 --> 00:20:20.052
So, if you reduce...

00:20:20.052 --> 00:20:23.063
If you use friction to reduce velocities

00:20:23.063 --> 00:20:24.113
after the solver step,

00:20:24.113 --> 00:20:26.903
it is very hard to bring
the rope to complete rest.

00:20:26.903 --> 00:20:30.423
Even if you have high
friction and you remove all

00:20:30.423 --> 00:20:31.623
the velocity from a node,

00:20:31.623 --> 00:20:33.823
if there is some external
force or constraint

00:20:33.823 --> 00:20:35.823
pulling on the node,
even just a little bit,

00:20:35.823 --> 00:20:38.187
it will move during the next solver step,

00:20:38.187 --> 00:20:40.987
because solver doesn't know
anything about the friction.

00:20:41.833 --> 00:20:45.253
So, we introduce the friction
constraint into the solver.

00:20:45.253 --> 00:20:46.086
It's very simple.

00:20:46.086 --> 00:20:48.573
You just need to remember the
original position at the start

00:20:48.573 --> 00:20:49.823
of the time step.

00:20:49.823 --> 00:20:52.263
So there's the p line
here, and the constraint

00:20:52.263 --> 00:20:55.753
is pulling the node back
towards that position.

00:20:55.753 --> 00:20:57.113
And we again split the effect

00:20:57.113 --> 00:20:59.633
into proportional and constant friction.

00:20:59.633 --> 00:21:01.843
And the constant friction
is basically what enables

00:21:01.843 --> 00:21:03.933
the effect of static friction.

00:21:03.933 --> 00:21:06.623
So if the node is within the radius Sfc

00:21:07.727 --> 00:21:09.109
from the original position,

00:21:09.109 --> 00:21:11.323
it will snap back to the original position

00:21:11.323 --> 00:21:13.349
each time this constraint is processed.

00:21:13.349 --> 00:21:16.543
And this coefficient, Sfc, Sfp are just

00:21:16.543 --> 00:21:18.123
another empirical coefficient that

00:21:18.123 --> 00:21:21.053
you will need to find some
values that work for you.

00:21:21.053 --> 00:21:23.253
But they are informed by
the Rope Tension analysis

00:21:23.253 --> 00:21:25.283
that I showed you on the previous slide.

00:21:26.913 --> 00:21:27.746
And here,

00:21:27.746 --> 00:21:30.343
I will just show you that the
friction works much better

00:21:30.343 --> 00:21:31.843
in these problematic cases.

00:21:43.093 --> 00:21:43.926
Okay.

00:21:43.926 --> 00:21:45.823
So, one topic that requires
some more attention

00:21:45.823 --> 00:21:48.571
is the convergence,
because it's not a great.

00:21:48.571 --> 00:21:51.666
First, if you have a long
rope, a lot of nodes,

00:21:51.666 --> 00:21:54.793
that goes around collision
and you pull on it

00:21:54.793 --> 00:21:55.883
on one end,

00:21:55.883 --> 00:21:58.033
it takes a lot of iterations
to propagate this impulse

00:21:58.033 --> 00:21:59.736
to the other end of the rope.

00:21:59.736 --> 00:22:03.099
Another good example is the
rope that's almost taut,

00:22:03.099 --> 00:22:05.573
like in this little sketch here.

00:22:05.573 --> 00:22:06.406
So in this case,

00:22:06.406 --> 00:22:09.303
the distance constraints are
pulling the node in direction

00:22:09.303 --> 00:22:10.493
that's almost perpendicular

00:22:10.493 --> 00:22:12.813
to the direction in
which we want it to move.

00:22:12.813 --> 00:22:14.913
So, they are very inefficient.

00:22:14.913 --> 00:22:16.173
And from my experience,

00:22:16.173 --> 00:22:17.723
you basically need a number of iterations

00:22:17.723 --> 00:22:20.363
that's proportional to the
number of nodes in the rope.

00:22:20.363 --> 00:22:21.893
But one iteration means going over

00:22:21.893 --> 00:22:23.653
all the constraints in the rope.

00:22:23.653 --> 00:22:26.613
And that makes the cost of
the simulation quadratic

00:22:26.613 --> 00:22:28.743
in terms of the number of nodes.

00:22:28.743 --> 00:22:30.193
And it can be very expensive.

00:22:31.453 --> 00:22:33.873
So, what can we do to improve that?

00:22:33.873 --> 00:22:36.383
One common thing to do is over-relaxation.

00:22:36.383 --> 00:22:39.373
So basically, I'm iterating
over each distance constraint.

00:22:39.373 --> 00:22:42.093
We will move the nodes
a little bit farther.

00:22:42.093 --> 00:22:43.093
Or in other words,

00:22:43.093 --> 00:22:45.671
we pretend as if the target
distance is a little bit shorter

00:22:45.671 --> 00:22:47.643
than what it really is.

00:22:47.643 --> 00:22:49.953
And that helps to reduce
the stretch in the rope

00:22:49.953 --> 00:22:51.293
a little bit faster.

00:22:51.293 --> 00:22:52.613
And as long as you keep

00:22:52.613 --> 00:22:54.913
the relaxation factor ɷ conservative,

00:22:54.913 --> 00:22:56.243
it should be still stable.

00:22:56.243 --> 00:22:57.543
At least in my experience.

00:22:58.473 --> 00:22:59.933
Other common method is multigrid.

00:22:59.933 --> 00:23:03.533
So, you create a series of
successively coarser levels

00:23:03.533 --> 00:23:05.113
of discretization.

00:23:05.113 --> 00:23:07.593
And you start on solving
on the coarsest level.

00:23:07.593 --> 00:23:08.843
You run a few iterations.

00:23:08.843 --> 00:23:10.833
Go to the next level, et cetera.

00:23:10.833 --> 00:23:14.113
And this helps enormously
with the convergence.

00:23:14.113 --> 00:23:16.143
Without it, if you have a
rope that's almost taut,

00:23:16.143 --> 00:23:19.173
it basically, feels like
it would never converge.

00:23:19.173 --> 00:23:22.913
And one note here is that we
had to mark nodes that are

00:23:22.913 --> 00:23:23.873
at the collision edge.

00:23:23.873 --> 00:23:27.363
And those had to be part
of the coarsest level,

00:23:27.363 --> 00:23:28.883
because if you skip them,

00:23:28.883 --> 00:23:31.093
the multigrid will start
pulling the rope into collision

00:23:31.093 --> 00:23:33.073
and you negate the form, basically.

00:23:33.073 --> 00:23:36.273
And then you will have
artifacts in the final results,

00:23:36.273 --> 00:23:38.203
where the rope bends in unnatural way.

00:23:39.673 --> 00:23:41.613
So, that helped but the rope simulation

00:23:41.613 --> 00:23:44.283
was still expensive for the budget we had.

00:23:44.283 --> 00:23:47.523
Even have a profile
capture on PS4 of some case

00:23:47.523 --> 00:23:49.433
where the rope was really expensive.

00:23:49.433 --> 00:23:51.573
And you can see it really sticks out here.

00:23:51.573 --> 00:23:54.493
So, just the solver step
is almost 12 milliseconds,

00:23:54.493 --> 00:23:57.333
and we only have six cores on PS4.

00:23:57.333 --> 00:23:58.166
So, if the rope takes
over one core for like,

00:23:58.166 --> 00:24:00.933
half the frame, that's just not good.

00:24:02.423 --> 00:24:04.763
So, what do you do when
you run out of ideas

00:24:04.763 --> 00:24:06.553
of how we will optimize something?

00:24:06.553 --> 00:24:10.603
Yes. You move it to GPU and
it's not the greatest fit.

00:24:10.603 --> 00:24:13.613
Because, as I was saying,
rope solving is inherently

00:24:13.613 --> 00:24:16.076
very serial process, as
you're propagating impulses

00:24:16.076 --> 00:24:17.153
along the rope.

00:24:18.115 --> 00:24:21.093
We're on the one wavefront
and process 64 nodes

00:24:21.093 --> 00:24:23.993
at the time, but it's not
the same as doing 64 nodes,

00:24:23.993 --> 00:24:25.693
one after each other, serially.

00:24:25.693 --> 00:24:26.913
So in total,

00:24:26.913 --> 00:24:29.953
you end up doing more work on
GPU than you would do on CPU.

00:24:31.113 --> 00:24:33.323
This is not the best
utilization of your silicon,

00:24:33.323 --> 00:24:34.423
but we still take it.

00:24:34.423 --> 00:24:36.263
And this is why.

00:24:36.263 --> 00:24:37.438
So here, you have a GPU profile capture,

00:24:37.438 --> 00:24:39.203
and it's not much faster.

00:24:39.203 --> 00:24:40.963
It takes almost 10 milliseconds

00:24:40.963 --> 00:24:43.413
for the compute shader to finish,

00:24:43.413 --> 00:24:45.543
but the main difference
and why this is much better

00:24:45.543 --> 00:24:49.377
is that it's just one bay front
running on one compute unit.

00:24:49.377 --> 00:24:51.883
The PS4 GPU has 18 computing needs.

00:24:51.883 --> 00:24:53.953
And if you make sure your
shared resource usage

00:24:53.953 --> 00:24:56.233
is not too high, several shaders may share

00:24:56.233 --> 00:24:57.303
the same compute unit.

00:24:57.303 --> 00:25:00.033
So, you're really just taking
tiny fracture of the GPU.

00:25:06.992 --> 00:25:09.530
Saying, you have a
collision plan and you need

00:25:09.530 --> 00:25:11.223
to push the node out.

00:25:11.223 --> 00:25:12.056
But in real level,

00:25:12.056 --> 00:25:14.733
you will have to deal with
some complex geometry,

00:25:14.733 --> 00:25:16.556
probably some triangle, triangle mesh,

00:25:16.556 --> 00:25:20.953
or maybe just pile of triangles,
or some convex shapes,

00:25:20.953 --> 00:25:23.453
but you still do want
to express this geometry

00:25:23.453 --> 00:25:25.913
in some simple and compact form.

00:25:25.913 --> 00:25:28.793
It can be about it quickly
because you're going to iterate

00:25:28.793 --> 00:25:30.313
many times over many nodes.

00:25:31.443 --> 00:25:34.503
So, what we did is we tried
to boil down all the geometry

00:25:34.503 --> 00:25:37.783
around each node into a
set of collision planes.

00:25:37.783 --> 00:25:39.913
And then, we needed to be able
to collide with convex edges.

00:25:39.913 --> 00:25:41.773
So, we just marked the pairs of planes

00:25:41.773 --> 00:25:43.733
that form a convex edge.

00:25:43.733 --> 00:25:46.143
And each node has maximum
of four collision planes

00:25:46.143 --> 00:25:47.613
and three convex edges.

00:25:47.613 --> 00:25:49.923
So, this sketch here literally represents,

00:25:50.863 --> 00:25:53.173
it's like example of
maximum geometry complexity

00:25:53.173 --> 00:25:55.233
that could be accurately expressed

00:25:55.233 --> 00:25:57.503
with the collision
constraints for one node.

00:25:59.223 --> 00:26:01.539
And this second sketch shows the edge one,

00:26:01.539 --> 00:26:04.063
when we're looking along the edge.

00:26:04.063 --> 00:26:05.863
And it shows what the
collision constraint is doing

00:26:05.863 --> 00:26:09.133
for the pair of planes n₁, n₂ .

00:26:09.133 --> 00:26:10.593
So we have to...

00:26:10.593 --> 00:26:11.923
Since they form convex edge,

00:26:11.923 --> 00:26:14.173
we have to check where is
the center of the node.

00:26:14.173 --> 00:26:17.813
And if it's in the blue area
A₃, it glides with the edge.

00:26:17.813 --> 00:26:19.253
So, we have to push it away from the edge,

00:26:19.253 --> 00:26:21.777
otherwise, it collides
with one of the planes.

00:26:21.777 --> 00:26:26.293
And this way, the node can
slide smoothly across the edge.

00:26:28.643 --> 00:26:30.383
Here we have a debug little from the game

00:26:30.383 --> 00:26:32.273
with the node going
through a window corner

00:26:32.273 --> 00:26:33.853
with some fairly complex geometry.

00:26:33.853 --> 00:26:37.460
And the way this works is
that we first try to estimate

00:26:37.460 --> 00:26:41.300
how the node might move during
the upcoming solver step.

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:44.263
Based on its current
velocity, the direction.

00:26:44.263 --> 00:26:46.573
Individual neighboring
nodes might be pulling it

00:26:46.573 --> 00:26:50.603
based on how much the closest
key frame nodes have moved.

00:26:50.603 --> 00:26:53.883
And this gives us A-b-b around the node,

00:26:53.883 --> 00:26:56.433
and then we collect all
the geometry in that A-b-b.

00:26:57.373 --> 00:27:00.183
And then, we try to be smart
and distill these triangles

00:27:00.183 --> 00:27:03.213
into our limited collision constraint set.

00:27:03.213 --> 00:27:04.903
So, each triangle gives
us a collision plane.

00:27:04.903 --> 00:27:06.433
And then we see if some of these planes

00:27:06.433 --> 00:27:08.933
are similar so we can merge them together.

00:27:08.933 --> 00:27:12.083
We did the convex edges and
then using current position of

00:27:12.083 --> 00:27:12.916
the node,

00:27:12.916 --> 00:27:15.713
try to see which planes are
more important than others,

00:27:15.713 --> 00:27:18.883
or which ones maybe, we can
ignore completely because they

00:27:18.883 --> 00:27:20.633
are behind other planes, et cetera.

00:27:22.067 --> 00:27:24.423
Here's just a few examples of simple cases

00:27:24.423 --> 00:27:26.233
where this approach breaks down.

00:27:26.233 --> 00:27:29.813
So, we only have pairs of
planes scraping a convex edge.

00:27:29.813 --> 00:27:33.573
We don't have a concept of group
of convex planes like this,

00:27:33.573 --> 00:27:36.123
and it's not trivial to add
this kind of concept into the

00:27:36.123 --> 00:27:37.113
solver.

00:27:37.113 --> 00:27:39.323
So in this case, if you have a node here,

00:27:39.323 --> 00:27:41.523
the plane n₃ will pull
it on the wrong side

00:27:41.523 --> 00:27:44.123
of the collision and then n₁ and n₃

00:27:44.123 --> 00:27:46.413
starts to fight over the node.

00:27:46.413 --> 00:27:47.246
So in this case,

00:27:47.246 --> 00:27:49.543
the best thing to do is
just to discard plane n₃,

00:27:50.763 --> 00:27:53.953
but it's much harder to do this call.

00:27:53.953 --> 00:27:54.786
To make this call.

00:27:54.786 --> 00:27:55.843
If the node is somewhere here,

00:27:55.843 --> 00:27:58.173
because if it ends up moving to the left,

00:27:58.173 --> 00:28:00.103
it will collide with
the plane n₂ like this,

00:28:00.103 --> 00:28:02.353
and create what's called
the ghost collision.

00:28:03.373 --> 00:28:05.773
And here is another simple example.

00:28:05.773 --> 00:28:07.970
So, plane n₁ here doesn't
have any convex edge.

00:28:07.970 --> 00:28:10.963
So, it forks as an infinite plane.

00:28:10.963 --> 00:28:13.613
And again, it can create a
ghost collision like this.

00:28:14.869 --> 00:28:18.698
A lot of effort went into
this heuristics of compiling

00:28:18.698 --> 00:28:21.763
complex geometry into
limited set of constraints

00:28:21.763 --> 00:28:24.075
for each node and trying to make it work

00:28:24.075 --> 00:28:25.433
as robustly as possible.

00:28:25.433 --> 00:28:28.073
And it never got totally robust.

00:28:28.073 --> 00:28:30.548
Sometimes our geometry is very complex

00:28:30.548 --> 00:28:34.293
and the rope needs to
slide across it very fast.

00:28:36.293 --> 00:28:38.553
So, this really here was the crux

00:28:38.553 --> 00:28:40.473
of the Dynamic Rope Simulation.

00:28:40.473 --> 00:28:43.493
Fortunately, in Last of Us 2,
the area where the rope can be

00:28:43.493 --> 00:28:45.953
deployed is limited by the
fact that one end of the rope

00:28:45.953 --> 00:28:47.973
is always anchored somewhere.

00:28:47.973 --> 00:28:48.806
So in the end,

00:28:48.806 --> 00:28:51.193
we just had to test these areas properly,

00:28:51.193 --> 00:28:54.313
and go and clean up the
show in problematic places.

00:28:54.313 --> 00:28:56.077
Also, we have a system that allows us

00:28:56.077 --> 00:28:59.223
to take collision triangles
to either ignore rope

00:28:59.223 --> 00:29:01.523
or to ignore everything but the rope.

00:29:01.523 --> 00:29:03.903
So, we can create special
layer of collision,

00:29:03.903 --> 00:29:06.113
of clean collision, just for the rope.

00:29:06.113 --> 00:29:08.363
If that's needed for some places.

00:29:09.783 --> 00:29:11.453
Shipping a game where you
can do this with a rope,

00:29:11.453 --> 00:29:13.953
anywhere in your level
would be much, much harder.

00:29:15.373 --> 00:29:18.153
And so, before we finish
the Dynamic Rope part,

00:29:18.153 --> 00:29:20.473
I just want to show
you a few useful tricks

00:29:20.473 --> 00:29:22.453
you can do with collision constraints.

00:29:22.453 --> 00:29:24.233
So, before you send
them off to the solver,

00:29:24.233 --> 00:29:26.793
you can look at them, and
see if there is something

00:29:26.793 --> 00:29:29.463
suspicious about them
that could cause issues.

00:29:29.463 --> 00:29:31.333
Here, we have a node with
two collision planes.

00:29:31.333 --> 00:29:34.233
The orange is normal;
indicates the positive side.

00:29:34.233 --> 00:29:37.376
So the node is basically
trapped between the planes.

00:29:37.376 --> 00:29:40.494
And this would usually happen
when some prop or character

00:29:40.494 --> 00:29:43.953
collision would animate
into static collision.

00:29:43.953 --> 00:29:46.513
Most often, from Ellie's tie or torso,

00:29:46.513 --> 00:29:49.043
and it's important for us to
never let the rope clip into

00:29:49.043 --> 00:29:49.876
the static collision.

00:29:49.876 --> 00:29:52.453
We don't care that much
about the character capsules.

00:29:52.453 --> 00:29:54.623
So, let's say the black
plane is the important one

00:29:54.623 --> 00:29:56.523
and the purple, less important.

00:29:57.923 --> 00:29:59.563
So, first, we'll project the node out

00:29:59.563 --> 00:30:00.033
of the black collision,

00:30:08.543 --> 00:30:10.513
This pinching prevention is pretty common

00:30:10.513 --> 00:30:12.193
in clothes simulations, I think.

00:30:15.433 --> 00:30:17.993
There's another useful trick
you can do during constraint

00:30:17.993 --> 00:30:19.533
pre-processing.

00:30:19.533 --> 00:30:21.533
I'm going to show you the problem first.

00:30:22.601 --> 00:30:25.653
It was mostly affecting weapon
straps because characters

00:30:25.653 --> 00:30:28.443
can sometimes animate their
hands through collision.

00:30:28.443 --> 00:30:31.553
And if they're handling a
baton that has strap on it,

00:30:31.553 --> 00:30:34.333
it can get stuck on a
collision and stretch out.

00:30:35.943 --> 00:30:38.063
It could get pretty severe.

00:30:39.973 --> 00:30:43.243
I've seen weapons straps
off from enemy weapons

00:30:43.243 --> 00:30:46.313
stretched across the
whole level a few times.

00:30:46.313 --> 00:30:48.963
Fortunately, there is a
simple trick to prevent that.

00:30:50.483 --> 00:30:53.663
Here we have a piece of
rope with four nodes.

00:30:53.663 --> 00:30:56.013
The yellow node 0 is key framed.

00:30:56.013 --> 00:30:57.223
And we know the length of the rope

00:30:57.223 --> 00:30:58.583
between node 0 and 3.

00:30:59.443 --> 00:31:03.553
And here we have a collision
constraint for the node 3.

00:31:03.553 --> 00:31:05.853
And we can just measure
the distance of this plane

00:31:05.853 --> 00:31:07.813
from the keyframed node.

00:31:07.813 --> 00:31:11.473
And if it's bigger than
the length of the rope,

00:31:11.473 --> 00:31:14.539
then we know this plane
will stretch out the rope,

00:31:14.539 --> 00:31:16.533
and we can just ignore this plane.

00:31:18.543 --> 00:31:20.163
It's very simple but effective.

00:31:21.323 --> 00:31:22.223
Here's the result.

00:31:25.776 --> 00:31:28.253
You can see how the
constraints are being removed

00:31:28.253 --> 00:31:30.353
as the strap starts to stretch.

00:31:35.153 --> 00:31:35.986
Okay.

00:31:35.986 --> 00:31:38.223
So now, we will jump
straight into the third part

00:31:38.223 --> 00:31:39.913
of this talk, which is going to be about

00:31:39.913 --> 00:31:41.933
the Taut Rope Simulation.

00:31:41.933 --> 00:31:45.153
And as a reminder, this
video shows what we're after.

00:31:45.153 --> 00:31:48.033
So, it's just a line that's
able to collide with collision

00:31:48.033 --> 00:31:51.853
and wrap around it, and
slide along the edges

00:31:51.853 --> 00:31:55.213
to maintain the shortest
distance between the end points.

00:31:55.213 --> 00:31:57.533
The collision needs to
be fully continuous.

00:31:57.533 --> 00:31:59.953
So, no matter how fast
the end points of the rope

00:31:59.953 --> 00:32:02.463
are moving, or how 10
other collision features,

00:32:03.533 --> 00:32:05.903
it never clips through the collision.

00:32:05.903 --> 00:32:09.334
And surprisingly, I didn't
find any previous work

00:32:09.334 --> 00:32:11.273
on something similar.

00:32:11.273 --> 00:32:13.753
So, I'm just going to show
you the approach that we took.

00:32:14.883 --> 00:32:17.303
So, we start with this simple case.

00:32:17.303 --> 00:32:19.933
We have just a straight line segment A-B.

00:32:19.933 --> 00:32:21.733
The game tells us that on the next frame,

00:32:21.733 --> 00:32:25.443
the end points A-B have to
move to these new positions.

00:32:25.443 --> 00:32:26.863
So, that's our first movement phase.

00:32:26.863 --> 00:32:28.863
That's where we want the points to move.

00:32:29.723 --> 00:32:32.603
And next, we need to check for collisions.

00:32:32.603 --> 00:32:35.773
And we do that by always moving
just one point at a time.

00:32:35.773 --> 00:32:39.993
So first, we move point A
and keep the point B fixed.

00:32:39.993 --> 00:32:42.856
And during that movement,
this line segment will sweep

00:32:42.856 --> 00:32:45.243
this green triangle.

00:32:45.243 --> 00:32:47.733
So, we just need to check for
intersection of this triangle

00:32:47.733 --> 00:32:50.724
with any neighboring convex edges.

00:32:50.724 --> 00:32:52.883
There is no collision here.

00:32:52.883 --> 00:32:54.383
So, the point A can move.

00:32:54.383 --> 00:32:56.533
And then, the middle is for point B.

00:32:56.533 --> 00:32:57.983
And here the triangle intersects

00:32:57.983 --> 00:33:00.093
with the blue convex edge.

00:33:00.093 --> 00:33:02.743
And the intersection point
is the point of contact.

00:33:02.743 --> 00:33:07.053
So we only let the point B move
up until the contact point.

00:33:07.053 --> 00:33:10.428
And we enter new edge point
into the rope and we can call it

00:33:10.428 --> 00:33:11.343
point C.

00:33:12.373 --> 00:33:16.595
And since this collision
face found a contact,

00:33:16.595 --> 00:33:19.303
we have to be able to
terminate a collision phase.

00:33:19.303 --> 00:33:21.753
And we go back to another movement phase.

00:33:21.753 --> 00:33:24.773
And the point B still has
not reached the target point.

00:33:24.773 --> 00:33:27.223
So, that will be the new
movement for that point.

00:33:28.613 --> 00:33:31.043
And I'm going to move the
camera so that you can hopefully

00:33:31.043 --> 00:33:32.953
see better next steps.

00:33:32.953 --> 00:33:34.193
So from this angle,

00:33:34.193 --> 00:33:36.473
you can see that the rope
is not straightened up,

00:33:36.473 --> 00:33:39.083
and we will want to let
the point C slide down

00:33:39.083 --> 00:33:42.216
along the collision edge
to straighten up the rope.

00:33:42.216 --> 00:33:45.393
Now, how do we find out
how far it should slide?

00:33:45.393 --> 00:33:48.113
So here we are, looking
along the edge and rA, rB

00:33:48.113 --> 00:33:51.033
are perpendicular distances
of the points from the edge.

00:33:52.493 --> 00:33:55.501
And we can rotate point A into the plane

00:33:55.501 --> 00:33:58.333
where the edge and the point B are.

00:33:59.433 --> 00:34:01.733
And here, we are looking onto the plane.

00:34:01.733 --> 00:34:04.473
Here, we can just draw
the straight line between

00:34:04.473 --> 00:34:07.373
the point B and A line
to find the intersection

00:34:07.373 --> 00:34:08.483
with the edge.

00:34:08.483 --> 00:34:10.733
And that's where we want
the point C to move.

00:34:11.903 --> 00:34:13.513
And if you go back to the 3D view,

00:34:13.513 --> 00:34:16.878
so now, we know how far
we want to let the point C

00:34:16.878 --> 00:34:19.403
slide along the edge.

00:34:19.403 --> 00:34:21.949
But this was just a movement phase.

00:34:21.949 --> 00:34:25.643
So now, we know where we
want points B and C to go,

00:34:27.143 --> 00:34:29.899
and we need to go to do
another collision phase

00:34:29.899 --> 00:34:32.543
to check if we actually can get there.

00:34:32.543 --> 00:34:34.623
And we do it again by
moving points one by one.

00:34:34.623 --> 00:34:36.303
So first, we move point C,

00:34:36.303 --> 00:34:39.223
and we need to check for
intersection of edges with these

00:34:39.223 --> 00:34:40.843
two triangles.

00:34:40.843 --> 00:34:41.676
It's all good.

00:34:41.676 --> 00:34:43.853
And then we check this
triangle for point B.

00:34:45.343 --> 00:34:47.223
No collision here.

00:34:47.223 --> 00:34:49.655
And since this collision phase
ended with no new contact

00:34:49.655 --> 00:34:53.043
we are done, and this is the
final position of the rope.

00:34:53.043 --> 00:34:55.353
So, this is the core of the algorithm.

00:34:55.353 --> 00:34:58.853
We are alternating these
movement and collision phases.

00:34:58.853 --> 00:35:00.033
Movement phase tells us where
we want points on the rope

00:35:01.843 --> 00:35:03.043
to move.

00:35:03.043 --> 00:35:05.933
And then, the collision
phase checks for collisions.

00:35:05.933 --> 00:35:07.193
And if there is a collision,

00:35:07.193 --> 00:35:09.903
we have to go back to another
movement phase to generate new

00:35:09.903 --> 00:35:11.363
target positions, et cetera.

00:35:12.293 --> 00:35:15.133
And if the collision phase
reports no new contacts,

00:35:15.133 --> 00:35:16.143
then we are done.

00:35:18.983 --> 00:35:21.513
I'm going to show you one
more example to explain better

00:35:21.513 --> 00:35:23.063
the movement phase.

00:35:23.063 --> 00:35:25.963
So, here we have a rope that
has two edge points already.

00:35:27.138 --> 00:35:30.553
And now the game tells us that
the end points have to move

00:35:30.553 --> 00:35:32.363
to these new positions.

00:35:32.363 --> 00:35:34.943
And it's not obvious how we
should directly calculate

00:35:34.943 --> 00:35:36.733
how far should the two middle points,

00:35:36.733 --> 00:35:40.773
B and C move along their edges
to straighten up the rope.

00:35:40.773 --> 00:35:44.003
So, we will only use the
first three points A, B, C.

00:35:44.003 --> 00:35:46.253
to find out where the point B should move

00:35:46.253 --> 00:35:48.963
to straighten up the segment A, B, C.

00:35:48.963 --> 00:35:51.443
Then we do the same for the B, C, D.

00:35:51.443 --> 00:35:53.403
to the move the point C.

00:35:53.403 --> 00:35:57.779
And then, we keep doing that
until we find a position

00:35:57.779 --> 00:36:01.163
where the rope is straighten
up or as straight as it can go,

00:36:01.163 --> 00:36:03.073
given the collision edges.

00:36:03.073 --> 00:36:05.523
So, the movement phase
is an iterative process.

00:36:06.723 --> 00:36:09.023
And there's also just one movement phase.

00:36:09.023 --> 00:36:12.753
So, as long as each point
on the rope is moving along

00:36:12.753 --> 00:36:15.333
the same edge and
doesn't change direction.

00:36:15.333 --> 00:36:17.063
You can keep iterating
and they'll be fine.

00:36:17.063 --> 00:36:20.873
The target position that
straightens up the rope.

00:36:20.873 --> 00:36:23.654
And now we would have to go
back before the collision phase

00:36:23.654 --> 00:36:26.133
to actually check if we can get there.

00:36:28.873 --> 00:36:30.983
So, there's one problem with
this iterative advancement

00:36:30.983 --> 00:36:32.173
of rope edge points.

00:36:33.083 --> 00:36:35.633
I'm going to show it on
this piece of geometry.

00:36:35.633 --> 00:36:37.823
So now, the upper point
has moved to the right,

00:36:37.823 --> 00:36:40.693
and now, we need to slide
all these edge points along

00:36:40.693 --> 00:36:42.093
their edges.

00:36:42.093 --> 00:36:44.473
But as the edges are getting
closer and closer together.

00:36:44.473 --> 00:36:46.733
The iterative method is getting
less and less effective,

00:36:46.733 --> 00:36:50.213
until the points pretty much
stop advancing completely.

00:36:50.213 --> 00:36:52.413
Fortunately, that is a nice
way to deal with this problem,

00:36:52.413 --> 00:36:55.353
since you can basically
unroll it all into 2D,

00:36:55.353 --> 00:36:56.553
and solve it in a plane.

00:36:57.533 --> 00:36:59.381
So, as you go along the rope,

00:36:59.381 --> 00:37:03.533
as long as two consecutive
edges are lying in a plane,

00:37:03.533 --> 00:37:06.203
you can keep unrolling
onto the same 2D plane.

00:37:06.203 --> 00:37:08.283
So here, we start with edges A and B,

00:37:08.283 --> 00:37:10.203
and their plane will be our drawing plane

00:37:10.203 --> 00:37:11.773
on the right.

00:37:11.773 --> 00:37:14.723
And first, we bring the
point P₀ into this plane

00:37:14.723 --> 00:37:16.453
by rotating it around edge A.

00:37:17.663 --> 00:37:20.243
And then, since B and C
are parallel to each other,

00:37:20.243 --> 00:37:22.793
we can bring edge E into this plane also

00:37:22.793 --> 00:37:25.406
by rotating it around edge B.

00:37:25.406 --> 00:37:28.293
And then, we do the same for D and E.

00:37:28.293 --> 00:37:31.083
And in the end, we also bring
point P₁ into this plane

00:37:31.083 --> 00:37:32.733
by rotating it around edge E.

00:37:33.843 --> 00:37:36.313
And so, once we have
all this in one plane,

00:37:36.313 --> 00:37:39.073
we can just draw a straight
line between P₀ and P₁.

00:37:39.913 --> 00:37:42.603
And in 2D, it's easy to
find these intersections.

00:37:42.603 --> 00:37:44.323
And then we bring it back into 3D.

00:37:44.323 --> 00:37:46.003
As we can see on the left side.

00:37:46.933 --> 00:37:49.863
So, only case when you can't
use this trick is when two

00:37:49.863 --> 00:37:52.563
consecutive edges on the
rope are askew lines.

00:37:52.563 --> 00:37:56.263
In this case, you still need
to use the iterative method.

00:37:58.763 --> 00:38:01.183
Okay. So that wasn't that bad.

00:38:01.183 --> 00:38:02.913
It gets worse when we
have to start dealing

00:38:02.913 --> 00:38:05.093
with intersection of edges.

00:38:05.093 --> 00:38:08.533
So here, we have this a
rather ugly piece of geometry,

00:38:08.533 --> 00:38:12.093
but it will serve us as a
good showcase to demonstrate

00:38:12.093 --> 00:38:14.923
how to deal with edge intersections.

00:38:14.923 --> 00:38:16.323
So, our rope has one edge point.

00:38:16.323 --> 00:38:18.539
And as the end points are
moving, it slides along

00:38:18.539 --> 00:38:21.443
the edge until our
collision phase reports that

00:38:21.443 --> 00:38:23.913
we hit some collision at this point.

00:38:23.913 --> 00:38:24.763
And here,

00:38:24.763 --> 00:38:27.743
the important thing here is
that our collision detection

00:38:27.743 --> 00:38:31.053
algorithm has to be able to
report all the edges that come

00:38:31.053 --> 00:38:33.153
together at this intersection point.

00:38:33.993 --> 00:38:35.663
So, we don't want just the first edge,

00:38:35.663 --> 00:38:38.653
or numerically, the first
edge that the rope hits.

00:38:38.653 --> 00:38:39.696
We want to be able to gather all the edges

00:38:39.696 --> 00:38:43.623
within some tolerance
of the final position.

00:38:44.673 --> 00:38:45.506
And this kind of numerical robustness

00:38:45.506 --> 00:38:49.263
is important part of the
whole Taut Rope Simulation.

00:38:49.263 --> 00:38:51.063
I'll talk about it later a bit more.

00:38:52.143 --> 00:38:54.583
Okay. So, we have this point on the rope

00:38:54.583 --> 00:38:57.113
that's at intersection
of five different edges.

00:38:57.113 --> 00:38:58.913
Now, what should happen next, right?

00:39:00.303 --> 00:39:01.136
Should...

00:39:01.136 --> 00:39:02.123
What is the point supposed to do?

00:39:02.123 --> 00:39:04.303
Should it slide along
some of the other edges,

00:39:04.303 --> 00:39:06.493
or should it stay in the intersection?

00:39:07.383 --> 00:39:09.753
So, to make it easier to reason about it,

00:39:09.753 --> 00:39:14.103
what we do is that we evaluate
all the pairs of two edges.

00:39:14.103 --> 00:39:16.783
So, we always only look
at two edges at a time,

00:39:16.783 --> 00:39:19.433
and we try to decide
what that intersection

00:39:19.433 --> 00:39:21.143
of two edges means.

00:39:21.143 --> 00:39:23.533
And then, we put it together at the end.

00:39:23.533 --> 00:39:26.143
So, before I can explain that better,

00:39:26.143 --> 00:39:29.913
we need to establish some
orientation for the rope.

00:39:29.913 --> 00:39:32.643
So, we choose one end of
the rope to be the origin

00:39:32.643 --> 00:39:34.023
or the beginning.

00:39:34.023 --> 00:39:36.673
And we, we establish the orientation.

00:39:36.673 --> 00:39:39.863
That it starts at the origin
and points toward the back.

00:39:39.863 --> 00:39:41.383
Toward the end, like that.

00:39:42.455 --> 00:39:44.403
And you also need an
orientation for the edges.

00:39:44.403 --> 00:39:46.893
So, we'll establish the
orientation like this.

00:39:47.893 --> 00:39:51.273
Which basically, means that if
you're standing on the phase

00:39:51.273 --> 00:39:54.463
that's facing the, the origin of the rope,

00:39:54.463 --> 00:39:56.617
that's the upper phase here,

00:39:56.617 --> 00:40:00.033
and you're looking along the
positive direction of the edge,

00:40:00.613 --> 00:40:04.293
then the rope has to pass the
edge on the right-hand side.

00:40:04.293 --> 00:40:05.883
Okay?

00:40:05.883 --> 00:40:09.813
Now, as I was saying, we
will be always evaluating

00:40:09.813 --> 00:40:10.793
one pair of edges.

00:40:10.793 --> 00:40:13.123
So here, we have two edges: A and B.

00:40:13.123 --> 00:40:15.193
And we found a plane in
which, these two edges slide.

00:40:15.193 --> 00:40:16.763
And that will be our drawing plane now.

00:40:16.763 --> 00:40:20.213
And we orient this plane in
a way that the rope is going

00:40:20.213 --> 00:40:22.733
from our side into the plane.

00:40:22.733 --> 00:40:26.743
So, I can draw the rope
crossing the plane like this.

00:40:26.743 --> 00:40:29.023
And we also have the
orientation of these two edges.

00:40:29.023 --> 00:40:30.043
And according to our rule,

00:40:30.043 --> 00:40:32.083
the rope has to pass
on the right-hand side

00:40:32.083 --> 00:40:34.103
of each of these edges.

00:40:34.103 --> 00:40:37.163
So, I know that the rope
has to be somewhere here.

00:40:37.163 --> 00:40:38.109
Okay?

00:40:38.109 --> 00:40:41.733
On the right-hand side of A,
and the right-hand side of B.

00:40:43.263 --> 00:40:45.743
And I drew the of edges
as line that are crossing

00:40:45.743 --> 00:40:47.203
each other, but in fact,

00:40:47.203 --> 00:40:50.093
they should either start
or end at the intersection.

00:40:50.093 --> 00:40:51.533
Otherwise, you will
have, like, a T-junction

00:40:51.533 --> 00:40:53.643
in the collision mesh, and
you're not going to worry

00:40:53.643 --> 00:40:55.079
about that case.

00:40:55.079 --> 00:40:58.104
So, depending on which edge starts or ends

00:40:58.104 --> 00:41:01.613
at the intersection, we
will have different cases.

00:41:01.613 --> 00:41:03.463
And the first case will be like this.

00:41:04.763 --> 00:41:08.403
And in 3D it would usually
look something like this.

00:41:08.403 --> 00:41:11.044
So, I call this case Inner
Corner because it's like a corner

00:41:11.044 --> 00:41:13.253
where the rope can get caught, basically.

00:41:14.293 --> 00:41:17.057
And next thing we do is,
considering where the previous

00:41:17.057 --> 00:41:19.112
and next point on the
rope are, we evaluate

00:41:19.112 --> 00:41:23.383
in which direction would the
rope slide along each edge,

00:41:23.383 --> 00:41:25.283
if we would only have that edge alone.

00:41:26.273 --> 00:41:28.773
So, we evaluate it first for edge A

00:41:28.773 --> 00:41:30.323
and then we do that for edge B.

00:41:31.473 --> 00:41:34.783
And different combinations of
these directions will give us

00:41:34.783 --> 00:41:37.543
different sub-cases for the Inner Corner.

00:41:37.543 --> 00:41:41.283
So, this sub-case basically
means the rope is trying to move

00:41:41.283 --> 00:41:44.653
into the corner, and I call
it Stable Inner Corner.

00:41:44.653 --> 00:41:48.163
And that's the case when the
rope stays at the intersection.

00:41:49.523 --> 00:41:51.255
And this one,

00:41:51.255 --> 00:41:55.053
this sub-case means that the
rope is a abandoning edge B,

00:41:55.053 --> 00:41:57.713
and it's moving or sliding along edge A

00:41:57.713 --> 00:41:59.583
out of the intersection.

00:41:59.583 --> 00:42:01.213
So, I call it Move Along A,

00:42:01.213 --> 00:42:02.813
and this would be Move Along B.

00:42:04.393 --> 00:42:05.273
And here.

00:42:05.273 --> 00:42:07.083
Here, we have this other case.

00:42:07.083 --> 00:42:10.793
And in 3D it would look
usually something like this.

00:42:10.793 --> 00:42:12.797
So, I call it Unstable Corner.

00:42:12.797 --> 00:42:15.739
And depending on which way we would...

00:42:15.739 --> 00:42:19.113
Which way would the rope
slide along each edge,

00:42:19.113 --> 00:42:20.573
we have this sub-case.

00:42:22.073 --> 00:42:24.933
Which, you can basically
choose A or B because the rope

00:42:24.933 --> 00:42:27.483
is on the vertex, basically,
and can go either way.

00:42:29.013 --> 00:42:31.903
And then, we have a Move
Along A or Move Along B.

00:42:33.773 --> 00:42:35.253
And then, we have this case.

00:42:35.253 --> 00:42:36.733
Where, if you think about it,

00:42:36.733 --> 00:42:40.123
the rope needs to be
on the right side of B,

00:42:40.123 --> 00:42:42.053
but the edge A will
not to let it go there.

00:42:42.053 --> 00:42:44.583
So, you can basically
disregard the edge B.

00:42:45.613 --> 00:42:48.413
In 3D, it would often
look something like this.

00:42:48.413 --> 00:42:51.143
So the edge B is basically
coming from the side,

00:42:51.143 --> 00:42:53.713
but the rope can't
really interact with it.

00:42:53.713 --> 00:42:55.743
I call this case Side Edge.

00:42:55.743 --> 00:42:57.573
I had to invent some
names for these cases.

00:42:57.573 --> 00:43:01.943
So, I can reason about them
and write a code for them.

00:43:01.943 --> 00:43:06.943
And the sub-cases would be
either Ignore B or Ignore A,

00:43:07.445 --> 00:43:08.883
in this other case.

00:43:10.553 --> 00:43:11.386
Okay.

00:43:11.386 --> 00:43:12.533
So, we're not done yet.

00:43:12.533 --> 00:43:15.803
So, bear with me for a little
longer through this section.

00:43:15.803 --> 00:43:19.633
The rope can cross the
plane of the edges twice.

00:43:19.633 --> 00:43:22.453
So, it comes from the origin,
it goes into the plane,

00:43:22.453 --> 00:43:24.069
and it comes back again.

00:43:24.069 --> 00:43:27.263
Geometrically, since the
rope is at the intersection,

00:43:27.263 --> 00:43:30.533
it really just touches the
plane at the intersection point.

00:43:30.533 --> 00:43:32.735
But from topological standpoint,
it goes into the plane,

00:43:32.735 --> 00:43:36.073
crosses the two edges, and comes back.

00:43:36.073 --> 00:43:37.043
And when it comes back,

00:43:37.043 --> 00:43:39.783
you'd have to be on the left-hand
side of each of the edges

00:43:39.783 --> 00:43:41.853
for the same reason that it had
to be on the right-hand side

00:43:41.853 --> 00:43:42.686
when going in.

00:43:43.693 --> 00:43:45.073
Um.

00:43:45.073 --> 00:43:47.423
So now, let's look at
what segments of the edges

00:43:47.423 --> 00:43:48.963
are we dealing with,
because that will give us

00:43:48.963 --> 00:43:50.483
different cases again.

00:43:50.483 --> 00:43:53.213
So first, let's consider this case.

00:43:53.213 --> 00:43:55.913
And maybe this corresponding
3D sketch will make it

00:43:55.913 --> 00:43:58.543
more obvious what's going on here.

00:43:58.543 --> 00:44:01.193
So basically, the rope was
going over the two edges,

00:44:01.193 --> 00:44:03.185
and now it's slid onto the corner.

00:44:03.185 --> 00:44:05.393
And it's about to slide off.

00:44:05.393 --> 00:44:07.593
Or maybe not, it depends
on the other edges

00:44:07.593 --> 00:44:09.373
coming into intersection.

00:44:09.373 --> 00:44:12.723
But for now, we're only
thinking about these two edges.

00:44:13.563 --> 00:44:16.333
I call this case Outer Corner.

00:44:16.333 --> 00:44:19.143
And the sub-cases are either

00:44:19.143 --> 00:44:21.393
the rope goes over A and then B,

00:44:21.393 --> 00:44:23.043
or first over B and then A.

00:44:24.183 --> 00:44:26.863
And finally, this last case,

00:44:26.863 --> 00:44:27.696
or this one.

00:44:27.696 --> 00:44:30.603
Those are the actual cases, basically.

00:44:30.603 --> 00:44:32.413
And in theory, it will
be something like this.

00:44:32.413 --> 00:44:36.313
So, the edges would have to be
like, relatively sharp edges

00:44:36.313 --> 00:44:38.973
so that the rope can
go over them like that.

00:44:38.973 --> 00:44:41.093
And depending on which way the rope

00:44:41.093 --> 00:44:43.653
wants to slide along each edge,

00:44:43.653 --> 00:44:45.403
we have these sub-cases,

00:44:45.403 --> 00:44:47.603
which are equivalent to
what you already saw before.

00:44:47.603 --> 00:44:49.413
So, we have a Stable Inner Corner,

00:44:49.413 --> 00:44:51.893
or Inner Corner Move
Along A, and Move Along B,

00:44:51.893 --> 00:44:54.313
or we can have also, Unstable Corner.

00:44:55.523 --> 00:44:56.356
Okay.

00:44:56.356 --> 00:44:59.843
So now, equipped with all these
topological categorization,

00:44:59.843 --> 00:45:00.033
we can look at our test case.

00:45:06.673 --> 00:45:10.193
So, we have these five
intersecting edges here,

00:45:10.193 --> 00:45:12.803
and we'll call them A, B, C, D, E.

00:45:12.803 --> 00:45:14.913
And we found out with what
their orientations are.

00:45:14.913 --> 00:45:16.693
And then, we have already
the topological case

00:45:16.693 --> 00:45:18.883
for each pair of the two edges.

00:45:18.883 --> 00:45:21.902
And we'll get, we'll get
this kind of metrics here.

00:45:21.902 --> 00:45:24.844
And first, we look for
some Side Edge cases,

00:45:24.844 --> 00:45:27.643
and we see that B and E are Side Edges.

00:45:27.643 --> 00:45:30.043
So, we can ignore these edges.

00:45:30.043 --> 00:45:32.923
And now we only have A, C, D left.

00:45:32.923 --> 00:45:35.683
And we see that there is an Inner Corner,

00:45:35.683 --> 00:45:38.723
Stable Inner Corner between A-C and A-D.

00:45:38.723 --> 00:45:40.265
And if there is any Stable Inner Corner,

00:45:40.265 --> 00:45:42.593
it means that the rope
is caught in the corner

00:45:42.593 --> 00:45:43.733
and stays there.

00:45:43.733 --> 00:45:46.653
So, we just keep the rope
point at the intersection.

00:45:46.653 --> 00:45:49.943
But the end points of the
rope will keep moving now.

00:45:49.943 --> 00:45:51.583
So, they move just a little bit,

00:45:51.583 --> 00:45:54.033
but the situation has changed slightly.

00:45:54.033 --> 00:45:56.272
So we have new metrics here.

00:45:56.272 --> 00:45:59.363
The edges E and B switched orientation,

00:45:59.363 --> 00:46:01.663
but they are still Side Edges.

00:46:01.663 --> 00:46:03.893
So, we can still ignore them.

00:46:03.893 --> 00:46:06.253
And it is no more Stable Inner Corner.

00:46:06.253 --> 00:46:07.933
So, the rope should move out now.

00:46:07.933 --> 00:46:10.503
And instead, we have
Inner Corner Move Along C,

00:46:10.503 --> 00:46:12.163
and Inner Corner Move Along D.

00:46:12.163 --> 00:46:13.933
So we know we will abandon edge A.

00:46:14.853 --> 00:46:18.903
And then also, edges C and D
create an Outer Corner C-D.

00:46:18.903 --> 00:46:22.913
So, we will want to split the
rope point into two points.

00:46:22.913 --> 00:46:24.243
First, will go along edge C.

00:46:24.243 --> 00:46:25.893
And second, will go along edge D.

00:46:27.333 --> 00:46:29.003
So, here it goes.

00:46:32.353 --> 00:46:34.873
And then, we can go back
and it would be basically

00:46:34.873 --> 00:46:37.323
the same thing but in reverse.

00:46:37.323 --> 00:46:40.223
So, at certain point, the two,

00:46:40.223 --> 00:46:42.783
the two rope points will
get within some tolerance

00:46:42.783 --> 00:46:44.953
from each other, and we will
merge them into one point.

00:46:44.953 --> 00:46:47.403
And then, we will reach the intersection,

00:46:47.403 --> 00:46:49.283
spend a while in the Stable Inner Corner,

00:46:49.283 --> 00:46:52.023
and then, Move Along A out of the corner.

00:46:53.783 --> 00:46:56.623
But even just for this
case of each intersection,

00:46:56.623 --> 00:46:59.341
the situation can change
dramatically depending on what

00:46:59.341 --> 00:47:01.533
the end points of the rope are doing.

00:47:01.533 --> 00:47:05.653
So just for illustration,
if rope ends go like this,

00:47:05.653 --> 00:47:09.023
then suddenly, edges A
and B are now forming

00:47:09.023 --> 00:47:10.133
an Outer Corner.

00:47:13.683 --> 00:47:17.193
And then, you can do this,
which is pretty common.

00:47:17.193 --> 00:47:20.953
Where the rope basically lies
within the face of a triangle.

00:47:20.953 --> 00:47:24.583
And then, when it reaches a
edge, it's parallel to the edge.

00:47:24.583 --> 00:47:27.843
So, we need to be able to
deal with these cases also.

00:47:30.963 --> 00:47:32.703
So, maybe this is a good moment

00:47:32.703 --> 00:47:35.203
to address this numerical
robustness I mentioned before.

00:47:35.203 --> 00:47:38.613
So when you do any kind
of distance tests, like,

00:47:38.613 --> 00:47:41.203
is the rope touching an edge
or two edges intersecting,

00:47:41.203 --> 00:47:43.803
you can't just test for some
distance being zero, right?

00:47:43.803 --> 00:47:47.153
You need to use some tolerance
and you want to be consistent

00:47:47.153 --> 00:47:47.986
with this tolerance.

00:47:47.986 --> 00:47:50.023
It has to go through the whole algorithm

00:47:50.023 --> 00:47:52.573
and becomes part of all
the collision tests,

00:47:52.573 --> 00:47:54.983
all the distance, or intersection tests.

00:47:54.983 --> 00:47:57.953
And we used tolerance of 0.001 millimeter,

00:47:57.953 --> 00:48:01.723
which is quite large, a necessity
for just numerical errors

00:48:01.723 --> 00:48:04.873
But that way, it also
covers geometric errors.

00:48:04.873 --> 00:48:06.433
If you, for example,

00:48:06.433 --> 00:48:09.023
have two boxes and you place
them next to each other

00:48:09.023 --> 00:48:13.073
in your, in your level
builder or level editor.

00:48:14.091 --> 00:48:17.834
It may be that when, when this
geometry comes into the game,

00:48:17.834 --> 00:48:20.673
the vertices that were aligning it,

00:48:20.673 --> 00:48:22.933
are not exactly at the
same coordinates, right?

00:48:22.933 --> 00:48:25.585
And you don't want your rope
to slip into the tiny slot

00:48:25.585 --> 00:48:27.143
between the boxes.

00:48:28.273 --> 00:48:32.690
And another problem is there
are some tests or predicates

00:48:32.690 --> 00:48:35.403
in this algorithm that are very important,

00:48:35.403 --> 00:48:37.743
but it can become a numerically unstable.

00:48:37.743 --> 00:48:39.473
With example is the actual orientation.

00:48:39.473 --> 00:48:40.723
It's important to get it right.

00:48:40.723 --> 00:48:42.773
Otherwise you basically tell
the rope to go on the wrong

00:48:42.773 --> 00:48:45.053
side of the, of the edge.

00:48:45.053 --> 00:48:46.993
But sometimes, like in
this case we just saw,

00:48:46.993 --> 00:48:49.213
when the rope is close
to parallel to an edge.

00:48:49.213 --> 00:48:51.913
There is no good way to tell
what the orientation should be.

00:48:51.913 --> 00:48:54.764
So, it's just important to tread carefully

00:48:54.764 --> 00:48:56.567
when writing these
predicates, and think about

00:48:56.567 --> 00:48:58.079
what happens in the singular cases,

00:48:58.079 --> 00:49:00.833
and what are the consequences.

00:49:00.833 --> 00:49:02.953
There is no time to go
into the more details,

00:49:02.953 --> 00:49:04.233
but I just thought I should bring it up

00:49:04.233 --> 00:49:07.204
because it definitely brings
another level of difficulty

00:49:07.204 --> 00:49:08.393
to the problem.

00:49:09.833 --> 00:49:11.543
And speaking of difficulties.

00:49:11.543 --> 00:49:15.323
So, the Taut Rope Simulation,
also never got 100% robust.

00:49:15.323 --> 00:49:19.083
To be able to deal with any
geometry that you throw at it.

00:49:19.083 --> 00:49:20.293
Here are the main reasons why.

00:49:20.293 --> 00:49:21.733
So, it's the numerical robust bugs there

00:49:21.733 --> 00:49:23.193
that I just mentioned.

00:49:23.193 --> 00:49:24.802
It's pretty hard to get that 200%

00:49:24.802 --> 00:49:27.855
in an algorithm that's fairly complex.

00:49:27.855 --> 00:49:31.093
The edge intersections
prove to be hard too.

00:49:31.093 --> 00:49:33.603
So, the topological
classification that I showed you,

00:49:33.603 --> 00:49:36.903
where we always just look
at one pair of edges,

00:49:36.903 --> 00:49:39.053
and then we also test in
which direction the rope

00:49:39.053 --> 00:49:41.123
wants to slide by only looking at, looking

00:49:41.123 --> 00:49:43.313
at one edge in isolation.

00:49:43.313 --> 00:49:44.803
So, it mostly works,

00:49:44.803 --> 00:49:48.953
but I did find some obscure
cases where there was some more

00:49:48.953 --> 00:49:51.203
intricate interdependency
between the edges

00:49:51.203 --> 00:49:52.753
at an intersection.

00:49:52.753 --> 00:49:55.713
At certain angle of the
rope going through it,

00:49:55.713 --> 00:49:58.673
and the algorithm would just
make the wrong decision.

00:50:02.093 --> 00:50:04.988
and we didn't have time and
the real need to dig deeper

00:50:04.988 --> 00:50:06.983
into all these cases.

00:50:06.983 --> 00:50:09.398
And one limitation, coming
just from code design,

00:50:09.398 --> 00:50:11.123
is that we only support maximum

00:50:11.123 --> 00:50:12.903
of eight edges per rope point.

00:50:12.903 --> 00:50:14.993
So, if you have a very
taxing error collision mesh

00:50:14.993 --> 00:50:17.793
with lot of convex edges coming together,

00:50:17.793 --> 00:50:19.733
and the rope needs to
slide over that vertex,

00:50:19.733 --> 00:50:22.803
it will just randomly
drop some of the edges.

00:50:22.803 --> 00:50:23.901
There is all this in this case,

00:50:23.901 --> 00:50:26.079
usually not catastrophic,
since we have already

00:50:26.079 --> 00:50:29.583
a lot of edges, and it will
keep the rope more or less

00:50:29.583 --> 00:50:30.416
on the surface.

00:50:33.233 --> 00:50:37.614
So, I'm going to show you
this scene from Uncharted 4.

00:50:37.614 --> 00:50:40.293
Drake wraps the winch
rope around the tree,

00:50:40.293 --> 00:50:41.363
as many times before,

00:50:41.363 --> 00:50:44.673
and now he's going to
drive up the mudslide.

00:50:46.833 --> 00:50:48.083
- [Drake] We're all set.

00:50:48.083 --> 00:50:49.164
Here we go.

00:50:49.164 --> 00:50:51.219
(mechanical whirring)

00:50:51.219 --> 00:50:53.220
- Easy.

00:50:53.220 --> 00:50:54.553
- [Sully] Uh-oh!

00:50:55.663 --> 00:50:57.282
- [Drake] Whoa, whoa, whoa!

00:50:57.282 --> 00:50:58.301
(car crashing)

00:50:58.301 --> 00:50:59.697
(boulders smashing)

00:50:59.697 --> 00:51:00.530
- Don't panic! Don't panic!

00:51:02.323 --> 00:51:03.953
- [Jaroslav Sinecky] So, did
you notice just what the rope

00:51:03.953 --> 00:51:06.163
was doing as the Jeep slid off?

00:51:06.163 --> 00:51:06.996
Of course you didn't.

00:51:06.996 --> 00:51:09.143
It's pretty much
impossible to see anything

00:51:09.143 --> 00:51:10.573
because it happens very fast,

00:51:10.573 --> 00:51:12.783
and there is a lot of particles.

00:51:12.783 --> 00:51:16.333
And the camera is not even
looking in that direction.

00:51:16.333 --> 00:51:17.166
Nevertheless,

00:51:17.166 --> 00:51:19.613
this was probably the hardest
case for the Taut Rope

00:51:19.613 --> 00:51:20.513
in the whole game.

00:51:21.433 --> 00:51:24.433
The reason it was hard is
because the rope is sliding

00:51:24.433 --> 00:51:26.133
very fast across lot of geometry.

00:51:26.133 --> 00:51:29.355
And since it's going mostly
parallel to the surface,

00:51:29.355 --> 00:51:32.803
it's colliding with all these
edges at very sharp angles

00:51:32.803 --> 00:51:34.933
and basically, hitting
all these edge cases

00:51:34.933 --> 00:51:36.533
and numerically unstable tests.

00:51:37.603 --> 00:51:39.783
And we could have failed
this particular case,

00:51:39.783 --> 00:51:42.793
as literally, nobody will be
seeing what the rope is doing.

00:51:42.793 --> 00:51:45.593
But it was kind of nice this
case and easy to reproduce.

00:51:46.823 --> 00:51:49.763
Here is a little more detail
and it goes step-by-step.

00:51:50.603 --> 00:51:51.436
And...

00:51:53.573 --> 00:51:56.733
So, if you pay attention
to this place here.

00:51:56.733 --> 00:51:57.566
Yeah.

00:51:57.566 --> 00:51:59.653
So, right now you can
see that the rope clipped

00:51:59.653 --> 00:52:02.263
into the collision, but it will recover.

00:52:02.263 --> 00:52:04.193
It will get pulled out again.

00:52:04.193 --> 00:52:06.713
And then there is another
problematic spot later.

00:52:06.713 --> 00:52:07.863
I think somewhere here.

00:52:08.733 --> 00:52:10.723
So, this is just to
show that not everything

00:52:10.723 --> 00:52:11.863
was a total success,

00:52:12.913 --> 00:52:14.725
but it also shows the general
tendency in these cases

00:52:14.725 --> 00:52:17.583
is that even if something
goes wrong locally,

00:52:17.583 --> 00:52:19.463
usually the other edges
with hold the rope up

00:52:19.463 --> 00:52:21.743
and pull it out of collision eventually.

00:52:21.743 --> 00:52:24.963
So, it rarely has a
catastrophic consequences

00:52:24.963 --> 00:52:25.813
for the gameplay.

00:52:27.233 --> 00:52:28.066
Fortunately.

00:52:29.833 --> 00:52:31.643
Okay. So, let's wrap it up.

00:52:31.643 --> 00:52:34.093
So, when Last of Us 2 came out,

00:52:34.093 --> 00:52:36.073
somebody noticed the
rope, and there was this

00:52:36.073 --> 00:52:38.483
long thread on Twitter
with also, reactions

00:52:38.483 --> 00:52:40.403
from game developers.

00:52:40.403 --> 00:52:42.543
And a lot of them saying that
seeing these was actually

00:52:42.543 --> 00:52:45.303
stressing them out and
giving them nightmares.

00:52:45.303 --> 00:52:47.713
Some people expressing their
fear that their producers

00:52:47.713 --> 00:52:50.003
will now ask for the same thing.

00:52:50.003 --> 00:52:52.963
So this talk, hopefully,
if somebody actually tries

00:52:52.963 --> 00:52:54.283
to implement something similar,

00:52:54.283 --> 00:52:56.569
will give him some pointers and help avoid

00:52:56.569 --> 00:52:59.546
some of the pitfalls that we went through.

00:52:59.546 --> 00:53:03.043
But also, I'm hoping this can
help make a better judgment

00:53:03.043 --> 00:53:06.253
of how difficult it is, and
if it's worth the effort.

00:53:06.253 --> 00:53:08.903
It can maybe serve as a
defense when some game director

00:53:08.903 --> 00:53:11.253
asks you for a rope like Last of Us 2.

00:53:12.343 --> 00:53:14.483
Because, yes, it wasn't easy.

00:53:14.483 --> 00:53:16.944
Over eight years in development.

00:53:16.944 --> 00:53:18.223
And even though the rope,

00:53:18.223 --> 00:53:20.363
definitely wasn't the
only thing I was doing

00:53:20.363 --> 00:53:21.293
during those years.

00:53:21.293 --> 00:53:24.733
It was by far, I would say, the hardest.

00:53:24.733 --> 00:53:25.983
But your mileage vary.

00:53:25.983 --> 00:53:27.833
You might not need everything we did.

00:53:28.903 --> 00:53:31.263
If you only need some visual
elements: hanging ropes,

00:53:31.263 --> 00:53:33.193
or vines, or weapon straps.

00:53:33.193 --> 00:53:35.863
Some simple rope PBD solver
is actually very easy

00:53:35.863 --> 00:53:38.143
to put together, as I showed.

00:53:38.143 --> 00:53:40.477
Now, depends if you need
the rope to be long,

00:53:40.477 --> 00:53:43.573
with a lot of nodes, because
then, maybe you will have to

00:53:43.573 --> 00:53:46.823
deal with convergence
and performance problems.

00:53:46.823 --> 00:53:49.573
How realistic you need the friction to be?

00:53:49.573 --> 00:53:51.013
How complex is the geometry?

00:53:51.943 --> 00:53:55.413
Because that's big one because
that hugely impacts how hard

00:53:55.413 --> 00:53:56.413
will the problem be.

00:53:57.398 --> 00:54:00.603
And not surprisingly,
if you have game play

00:54:00.603 --> 00:54:03.833
depending on your rope sim,
that's when things get hard.

00:54:03.833 --> 00:54:05.262
So for the Taut Rope,

00:54:05.262 --> 00:54:07.653
which drove all the
gameplay in Uncharted 4,

00:54:07.653 --> 00:54:08.543
and the Dynamic Rope,

00:54:08.543 --> 00:54:11.322
which we needed for
gameplay in Last of Us 2,

00:54:11.322 --> 00:54:13.833
proved to be hard problems.

00:54:13.833 --> 00:54:15.663
What I showed you for
the Taut Rope Sim may not

00:54:15.663 --> 00:54:18.235
seem very complicated,
but it is a lot of code,

00:54:18.235 --> 00:54:20.653
a lot of complicated bookkeeping,

00:54:20.653 --> 00:54:22.983
and dealing with edge cases,
and numerical robustness.

00:54:22.983 --> 00:54:25.903
So, don't underestimate that
part, because I sure did.

00:54:27.123 --> 00:54:29.673
And as I already intimated,

00:54:29.673 --> 00:54:32.893
even though significant effort
went into these systems,

00:54:32.893 --> 00:54:35.533
you never got them 100%
robust to be able to deal

00:54:35.533 --> 00:54:36.963
with just any geometry.

00:54:37.993 --> 00:54:38.826
So again,

00:54:38.826 --> 00:54:41.033
the geometric complexity
is the biggest factor here.

00:54:41.033 --> 00:54:43.393
So if you can limit, in any way,

00:54:43.393 --> 00:54:45.503
how complex is the geometry that the rope

00:54:45.503 --> 00:54:46.903
has to interact with.

00:54:46.903 --> 00:54:50.313
That's, that's the biggest
help you can give to yourself.

00:54:50.313 --> 00:54:52.503
You know, by limiting
the area where the rope

00:54:52.503 --> 00:54:55.394
can be deployed, or what
kind of collision features

00:54:55.394 --> 00:54:57.522
it needs to interact with.

00:54:57.522 --> 00:55:00.033
Maybe, getting some clean
and simple collision authored

00:55:00.873 --> 00:55:04.223
for these areas, or have some
offline pre-processing step

00:55:04.223 --> 00:55:05.573
to clean it up.

00:55:05.573 --> 00:55:07.263
So, all these things,

00:55:07.263 --> 00:55:09.906
it's definitely worth looking into.

00:55:09.906 --> 00:55:10.893
(tense dramatic music)

00:55:10.893 --> 00:55:12.623
- And one last piece of advice.

00:55:12.623 --> 00:55:15.173
So, if you're going to
implement something like that,

00:55:15.173 --> 00:55:16.006
it will not be easy.

00:55:16.006 --> 00:55:19.303
So, it's worth to invest
some time into creating

00:55:19.303 --> 00:55:22.567
some solid debugging tools
to make your life easier.

00:55:22.567 --> 00:55:25.433
One nice thing about the
rope simulation like that

00:55:25.433 --> 00:55:28.483
is that it's a relatively
isolatable piece of software.

00:55:28.483 --> 00:55:32.927
So, can grab a piece of
a DPDK memory and use it

00:55:32.927 --> 00:55:36.147
as a ring buffer, and store all your input

00:55:36.147 --> 00:55:38.343
and state for each frame.

00:55:38.343 --> 00:55:39.783
And then, if something goes wrong,

00:55:39.783 --> 00:55:43.342
you can pause, and step back,
and look at what failed.

00:55:43.342 --> 00:55:45.903
You bring up all your diagnostic drawing,

00:55:45.903 --> 00:55:46.869
and when stepping forward,

00:55:46.869 --> 00:55:50.563
you can run exactly the same simulation.

00:55:50.563 --> 00:55:52.813
Our engine is not
deterministic. Not even close.

00:55:52.813 --> 00:55:54.543
But the rope simulation is.

00:55:54.543 --> 00:55:57.623
There is nothing that would
be breaking determinism here.

00:55:57.623 --> 00:55:59.693
So, you can find a frame
when something went wrong,

00:55:59.693 --> 00:56:02.083
and then just play that
frame over and over,

00:56:02.083 --> 00:56:04.803
and debug it as you, as you want.

00:56:04.803 --> 00:56:07.257
We even created a Rope
Dump where if somebody,

00:56:07.257 --> 00:56:11.033
mostly testers, would
find something went wrong.

00:56:11.033 --> 00:56:12.693
They could just dump the ring buffer,

00:56:12.693 --> 00:56:14.853
plus some collision
shape data into a file.

00:56:14.853 --> 00:56:16.453
And then, I could load
it up on my Dev Kit,

00:56:16.453 --> 00:56:19.063
and debug it as I wanted.

00:56:20.303 --> 00:56:23.423
And so, this tool was absolutely crucial.

00:56:23.423 --> 00:56:26.333
You could never ship a game
with these rope features

00:56:26.333 --> 00:56:27.683
without the rope debugger.

00:56:29.953 --> 00:56:32.703
And that's the end of my talk.

00:56:32.703 --> 00:56:35.663
So presumably, I'm literally
answering your questions

00:56:35.663 --> 00:56:37.283
in the chat right now,

00:56:37.283 --> 00:56:40.093
but you can also contact me at this email

00:56:40.093 --> 00:56:44.933
if you have more questions,
or ideas, or comments.

00:56:44.933 --> 00:56:46.493
So, thank you for listening.

00:56:47.658 --> 00:56:51.408
(energetic electronic music)

